1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:18,018
A makeover project
for your most unhinged leftovers.

2
00:00:18,084 --> 00:00:21,888
The best chefs in Korea will
turn your fridge inside out.

3
00:00:21,955 --> 00:00:24,357
The classy cooking show has returned.

4
00:00:24,424 --> 00:00:28,228
<i>-Chef & My Fridge!</i>
<i>-My Fridge!</i>

5
00:00:29,863 --> 00:00:32,832
LET'S START ANOTHER DAY

6
00:00:32,899 --> 00:00:34,968
Our chefs seem cheerful today.

7
00:00:35,035 --> 00:00:37,170
-They look excited.
-Yes.

8
00:00:37,237 --> 00:00:40,140
-One of them braided her hair too.
-Yes.

9
00:00:40,206 --> 00:00:41,141
But why?

10
00:00:41,207 --> 00:00:43,676
-What's with that smile?
-The studio is buzzing.

11
00:00:43,743 --> 00:00:45,412
She's different with her braids.

12
00:00:45,478 --> 00:00:47,814
-Stop it!
-Why is she making that face?

13
00:00:47,881 --> 00:00:50,650
-She should braid her senses.
-Sorry.

14
00:00:50,717 --> 00:00:52,352
-It's a first.
-Let's change seats, Nam-no.

15
00:00:52,419 --> 00:00:54,788
-I keep seeing her.
-No, thanks.

16
00:00:54,854 --> 00:00:56,489
-I'm sorry.
-I can still see her.

17
00:00:56,556 --> 00:00:58,324
Anyway, she seems very excited.

18
00:00:58,391 --> 00:00:59,392
Let's begin.

19
00:00:59,459 --> 00:01:02,195
Please welcome today's fridge owner.

20
00:01:02,262 --> 00:01:03,897
Come on out!

21
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:08,401
WOW

22
00:01:10,770 --> 00:01:12,505
Today's fridge owner!

23
00:01:12,572 --> 00:01:15,241
-Please welcome actor Son Ye-jin!
-Hello.

24
00:01:15,308 --> 00:01:17,610
-It's great to be here.
-Welcome.

25
00:01:17,677 --> 00:01:19,412
Why don't you say hello to our viewers?

26
00:01:19,479 --> 00:01:22,449
Hello. I'm Son Ye-jin.

27
00:01:22,515 --> 00:01:25,285
It's hard to believe
that I'm sitting here right now,

28
00:01:25,351 --> 00:01:28,021
at my favorite show.

29
00:01:28,088 --> 00:01:30,423
I'm so happy to be here.

30
00:01:30,890 --> 00:01:33,326
THE HONOR IS OURS

31
00:01:34,994 --> 00:01:38,231
Ye-jin is someone who brings
that <i>Something in the Rain.</i>

32
00:01:38,298 --> 00:01:39,766
That drama was aired here.

33
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:41,234
And when she walks by,

34
00:01:41,301 --> 00:01:43,103
-Yes?
-…it smells like <i>Summer Scent.</i>

35
00:01:43,169 --> 00:01:44,337
THANK YOU

36
00:01:44,938 --> 00:01:46,072
MY KIND OF JOKE♥

37
00:01:46,139 --> 00:01:48,608
After you decided to join the show,

38
00:01:48,675 --> 00:01:51,611
I heard you felt a little unsettled.
Why is that?

39
00:01:51,678 --> 00:01:53,179
-Why?
-Well…

40
00:01:53,246 --> 00:01:55,548
This show is a lot of fun,

41
00:01:55,615 --> 00:01:58,184
so I thought it'd be fun to be here.

42
00:01:58,251 --> 00:02:03,289
But the fact that I had to share my fridge

43
00:02:03,356 --> 00:02:05,525
felt like showing my bare face on TV.

44
00:02:05,592 --> 00:02:09,095
-Right.
-Also, I'm not single, but married.

45
00:02:09,162 --> 00:02:11,698
So I began to worry,
thinking my fridge won't look great,

46
00:02:11,764 --> 00:02:14,334
then even began to make
side dishes I've never made before

47
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:15,702
and cleaned it out too.

48
00:02:15,768 --> 00:02:17,570
While I was organizing
everything yesterday,

49
00:02:17,637 --> 00:02:19,139
my husband said,

50
00:02:19,806 --> 00:02:22,442
"This isn't the fridge that I know."

51
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:23,676
Did he really?

52
00:02:23,743 --> 00:02:25,512
-Was it that different?
-I'm coming clean.

53
00:02:25,578 --> 00:02:27,213
She'd like to tell us now

54
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:29,015
that she did a bit of cleaning.

55
00:02:29,082 --> 00:02:30,917
Jung-hwan also became a bit excited

56
00:02:30,984 --> 00:02:33,186
after hearing that you'd be here.

57
00:02:33,253 --> 00:02:34,320
Me?

58
00:02:35,788 --> 00:02:37,790
You've worked with her husband before.

59
00:02:38,658 --> 00:02:40,827
When was that? Do you remember?

60
00:02:40,894 --> 00:02:42,929
-Early 2000s, right?
-Around then.

61
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:45,131
He came to France for an ad
because I couldn't leave.

62
00:02:45,431 --> 00:02:46,599
HE CAME ALL THE WAY TO FRANCE

63
00:02:46,666 --> 00:02:47,600
There was no other way.

64
00:02:48,234 --> 00:02:49,836
What a guy.

65
00:02:51,304 --> 00:02:52,906
I'm sorry.

66
00:02:52,972 --> 00:02:55,141
Everyone, here's an amazing fact.

67
00:02:55,208 --> 00:02:58,511
Ye-jin also has a connection to that ad.

68
00:02:58,578 --> 00:02:59,479
I do.

69
00:02:59,546 --> 00:03:02,015
Your official debut
in the entertainment business

70
00:03:02,081 --> 00:03:03,950
-was an ad for that brand, right?
-Yes.

71
00:03:04,017 --> 00:03:05,718
DEBUTED WITH THE AD FOR THE SAME BRAND

72
00:03:05,785 --> 00:03:08,988
I thought it was a fun coincidence.

73
00:03:09,055 --> 00:03:12,392
She has another special reason
for visiting us today.

74
00:03:12,458 --> 00:03:14,594
-Your new project, right?
-That's right.

75
00:03:14,661 --> 00:03:17,897
-It's a new Netflix series.
-A series.

76
00:03:17,964 --> 00:03:21,301
I'm playing a character named Lady Cho,

77
00:03:21,367 --> 00:03:24,571
someone who was almost too talented
to be born in the Joseon era.

78
00:03:24,637 --> 00:03:27,340
<i>Ji Chang-wook is playing</i>
<i>the biggest player of Joseon,</i>

79
00:03:27,407 --> 00:03:31,144
<i>and the two</i>
<i>get into a scandalous love bet.</i>

80
00:03:31,211 --> 00:03:34,547
That's the main storyline.

81
00:03:34,614 --> 00:03:36,149
-It premieres on September 18th?
-Yes.

82
00:03:36,216 --> 00:03:39,219
-Please watch it during Chuseok.
-I see.

83
00:03:39,285 --> 00:03:40,353
<i>The Scandal.</i>

84
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:42,155
Many of our chefs

85
00:03:42,222 --> 00:03:44,991
are fans of Ye-jin's work,

86
00:03:45,058 --> 00:03:46,726
-especially Chef Eun-yeong.
-Yes.

87
00:03:46,793 --> 00:03:48,861
-Really?
-You were once her inspo.

88
00:03:48,928 --> 00:03:49,996
"Inspo"?

89
00:03:50,063 --> 00:03:51,231
Her inspo?

90
00:03:51,798 --> 00:03:53,299
THAT'S RIGHT

91
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:55,935
THE INSPO TO REMEMBER

92
00:03:56,002 --> 00:03:57,070
Don't smile like that.

93
00:03:57,136 --> 00:03:59,305
You're straight in my view,
and it's too hard to look at.

94
00:03:59,706 --> 00:04:01,107
SMH…

95
00:04:01,174 --> 00:04:03,443
What did I do to deserve this seat?

96
00:04:03,509 --> 00:04:04,510
Is that <i>The Classic</i>?

97
00:04:04,577 --> 00:04:05,411
<i>Summer Scent.</i>

98
00:04:06,279 --> 00:04:09,249
-I did my hair like her in that drama.
-So?

99
00:04:09,315 --> 00:04:11,351
What about her became your inspo?

100
00:04:11,417 --> 00:04:13,086
-What's the inspo?
-Well, the inspo?

101
00:04:13,152 --> 00:04:15,455
Soft and demure.

102
00:04:15,521 --> 00:04:17,657
-And you're doing this?
-Soft and demure.

103
00:04:18,224 --> 00:04:20,460
Who shows "soft and demure" like this?

104
00:04:20,526 --> 00:04:22,929
-These are like high beams.
-You're right.

105
00:04:22,996 --> 00:04:24,497
Why did you clench your fists?

106
00:04:24,564 --> 00:04:27,267
-Are these flowers?
-So it was soft and demure.

107
00:04:27,333 --> 00:04:29,902
It's more like Summer Lunacy.

108
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:32,105
Summer Lunacy?

109
00:04:32,171 --> 00:04:34,507
-She's beautiful.
-Like the summer horror special.

110
00:04:34,574 --> 00:04:36,276
-She makes me nervous.
-Yes.

111
00:04:36,342 --> 00:04:38,578
Also, Chef Jong-won.

112
00:04:38,645 --> 00:04:40,413
He loves your work, Ye-jin.

113
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:43,683
I came here today
while listening to this film's soundtrack.

114
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:45,985
<i>A Moment to Remember.</i>

115
00:04:46,052 --> 00:04:47,553
I love that.

116
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:48,821
She was great there.

117
00:04:48,888 --> 00:04:50,923
She was the nation's first love.

118
00:04:50,990 --> 00:04:55,528
I couldn't wait for today
since hearing she'd be here.

119
00:04:55,595 --> 00:04:58,865
Following Chef Jong-won is Chef Nam-no.

120
00:04:58,931 --> 00:05:00,300
-He doesn't know what to do.
-Yes.

121
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,669
Why are you looking at me? Look at her.

122
00:05:02,735 --> 00:05:05,538
I can't look at her. She's too pretty.

123
00:05:05,605 --> 00:05:07,473
What are your memories of her?

124
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:11,144
-I only watch film noir, never romance.
-Film noir.

125
00:05:11,210 --> 00:05:13,680
But while watching <i>A Moment to Remember,</i>

126
00:05:13,746 --> 00:05:16,215
I ended up bawling.

127
00:05:16,516 --> 00:05:17,984
THANK YOU

128
00:05:18,051 --> 00:05:19,852
He only cries
when he gets his food taken away.

129
00:05:19,919 --> 00:05:22,955
-It was that sad.
-It was?

130
00:05:23,022 --> 00:05:24,157
Must've been tragic.

131
00:05:25,258 --> 00:05:27,060
<i>-Lovers' Concerto.</i>
<i>-Lovers' Concerto.</i>

132
00:05:27,126 --> 00:05:30,063
She was the textbook definition
of soft and demure.

133
00:05:30,129 --> 00:05:33,166
We can stay up all night
discussing her work.

134
00:05:33,232 --> 00:05:35,635
She also received many accolades.

135
00:05:35,702 --> 00:05:37,637
Daejong Film Award,
Blue Dragon Film Awards,

136
00:05:37,704 --> 00:05:38,871
and Baeksang Arts Awards.

137
00:05:38,938 --> 00:05:43,176
She received
the Best Actress Award for all three.

138
00:05:43,242 --> 00:05:45,278
-She's an acting genius, isn't she?
-Yes.

139
00:05:45,345 --> 00:05:47,313
-If it's okay with you…
-There's a recent trend

140
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:49,849
<i>-of acting challenges.</i>
<i>-Yes, it's the trend.</i>

141
00:05:49,916 --> 00:05:52,418
You use one line
to act out the curious version,

142
00:05:52,485 --> 00:05:54,153
the sad version,

143
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:55,355
the angry version,

144
00:05:55,421 --> 00:05:56,723
and a flirtatious version.

145
00:05:56,789 --> 00:05:59,792
-You'll act out four different versions.
-That's tough.

146
00:05:59,859 --> 00:06:04,197
We saw that Eun-yeong
has done this challenge.

147
00:06:04,263 --> 00:06:06,366
I like to stay up-to-date, so I tried it.

148
00:06:06,766 --> 00:06:08,000
CURIOUS

149
00:06:08,067 --> 00:06:10,303
-Can you show us? You've done it.
-Chef Eun-yeong.

150
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:12,505
-Let's go with "Have you eaten?"
-"Have you eaten?"

151
00:06:12,572 --> 00:06:13,840
-The curious version.
-The curious version.

152
00:06:14,574 --> 00:06:15,842
Have you eaten?

153
00:06:15,908 --> 00:06:18,311
-Did you eat?
-That's the curious version.

154
00:06:18,611 --> 00:06:20,580
IS THIS RIGHT?

155
00:06:20,646 --> 00:06:22,949
-She's nitpicking.
-Or threatening.

156
00:06:23,015 --> 00:06:24,817
-"Have you eaten?"
-"You ate?"

157
00:06:24,884 --> 00:06:27,220
-Was that curiosity?
-It was curiosity.

158
00:06:27,286 --> 00:06:29,355
-The sad version. Cue.
-The sad version.

159
00:06:29,422 --> 00:06:31,257
You've eaten, right?

160
00:06:33,326 --> 00:06:36,195
-You have to eat.
-Jeez.

161
00:06:36,262 --> 00:06:37,964
Such bad acting.

162
00:06:38,030 --> 00:06:39,499
-Not bad.
-Come on.

163
00:06:39,565 --> 00:06:41,367
-Give us the angry version.
-The angry version?

164
00:06:41,434 --> 00:06:42,969
Have you eaten?

165
00:06:43,035 --> 00:06:44,537
You've eaten?

166
00:06:44,604 --> 00:06:46,873
3. ANGRY

167
00:06:46,939 --> 00:06:48,808
-The flirtatious version.
-I can do that.

168
00:06:48,875 --> 00:06:50,476
Please! I don't want to see it.

169
00:06:50,543 --> 00:06:51,811
-No, not this.
-Wait.

170
00:06:51,878 --> 00:06:53,045
I'M NOT WATCHING THIS…

171
00:06:53,112 --> 00:06:54,080
Show us.

172
00:06:54,147 --> 00:06:55,248
Have you eaten?

173
00:06:57,950 --> 00:06:59,919
THE OPPOSITE SIDE GETS WIPED OUT

174
00:07:00,319 --> 00:07:01,320
SWOOSH…

175
00:07:01,387 --> 00:07:02,221
I'm glad I didn't see it.

176
00:07:02,288 --> 00:07:03,623
Was that for me?

177
00:07:03,689 --> 00:07:06,192
EXTREME FLIRTING WITH HER INSPO…

178
00:07:06,259 --> 00:07:08,494
Chef Eun-yeong, have you taken your meds?

179
00:07:09,495 --> 00:07:11,531
"Have you taken your meds?"

180
00:07:11,597 --> 00:07:13,166
-Have you?
-I have.

181
00:07:13,232 --> 00:07:15,067
Have you taken your meds?

182
00:07:15,134 --> 00:07:16,202
You haven't?

183
00:07:16,269 --> 00:07:18,237
-You haven't!
-You haven't taken it?

184
00:07:18,304 --> 00:07:19,238
Take your meds!

185
00:07:19,305 --> 00:07:21,174
-So you're taking meds.
-Take your meds.

186
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:24,444
-I took them late today.
-So those are the versions.

187
00:07:24,510 --> 00:07:26,646
-That wasn't easy.
-Right?

188
00:07:26,712 --> 00:07:28,114
-Will you give it a try?
-Quickly.

189
00:07:28,181 --> 00:07:30,349
I'll do it to Chef Hyun-suk.

190
00:07:30,416 --> 00:07:33,119
-Hyun-suk?
-I need a partner to do that.

191
00:07:33,619 --> 00:07:34,587
IN THAT CASE…

192
00:07:34,921 --> 00:07:36,189
GETTING READY

193
00:07:36,255 --> 00:07:38,224
He's excited that Ye-jin chose him.

194
00:07:38,658 --> 00:07:40,259
I'M READY

195
00:07:40,326 --> 00:07:41,461
Let's do it quickly.

196
00:07:41,527 --> 00:07:43,830
"Have you eaten?" The curious version.

197
00:07:43,896 --> 00:07:45,832
Have you eaten?

198
00:07:47,099 --> 00:07:48,134
…CAN'T HIDE HIS SMILE

199
00:07:48,201 --> 00:07:49,335
He's happy.

200
00:07:49,402 --> 00:07:50,403
Everything comes to the center.

201
00:07:52,104 --> 00:07:53,739
-Why are you so happy?
-He's excited.

202
00:07:53,806 --> 00:07:55,174
The sad version.

203
00:07:55,241 --> 00:07:56,909
Have you eaten?

204
00:07:56,976 --> 00:07:58,211
CHOKING UP…

205
00:07:58,277 --> 00:07:59,412
The angry version.

206
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:02,248
You've eaten?

207
00:08:03,149 --> 00:08:04,984
SCARED…

208
00:08:05,051 --> 00:08:06,018
That was different.

209
00:08:06,085 --> 00:08:07,820
The flirtatious version.

210
00:08:07,887 --> 00:08:09,589
Have you eaten?

211
00:08:09,655 --> 00:08:11,123
4. FLIRTATIOUS

212
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:12,158
Yes.

213
00:08:13,292 --> 00:08:14,460
TEE-HEE

214
00:08:14,894 --> 00:08:15,995
THE ROM-COM MASTER

215
00:08:16,062 --> 00:08:18,097
-She's different.
-His heart is racing.

216
00:08:18,431 --> 00:08:20,666
PIT-A-PAT

217
00:08:22,902 --> 00:08:24,170
That woke you up, right?

218
00:08:24,237 --> 00:08:26,105
Even the elderly have feelings.

219
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:28,541
THE OLD MAN AND THE HEART

220
00:08:28,608 --> 00:08:30,443
Watch out for high blood pressure.

221
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:31,777
She's such a pro.

222
00:08:31,844 --> 00:08:33,446
-That shouldn't have been easy.
-That was good.

223
00:08:33,513 --> 00:08:35,248
She's such a great actor.

224
00:08:35,314 --> 00:08:36,616
I hear that

225
00:08:36,682 --> 00:08:38,451
she's interested in comedy too.

226
00:08:38,518 --> 00:08:39,385
GLANCES

227
00:08:39,452 --> 00:08:41,520
One of her longtime hobbies

228
00:08:41,587 --> 00:08:43,256
is attempting to make people laugh.

229
00:08:43,322 --> 00:08:46,359
-I see.
-Pretty people have that hobby.

230
00:08:46,425 --> 00:08:47,894
I would know.

231
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:48,961
How do you know?

232
00:08:49,028 --> 00:08:50,897
-How do you know?
-How would you know?

233
00:08:50,963 --> 00:08:52,498
-How do you know?
-How?

234
00:08:52,565 --> 00:08:53,733
HE-HE

235
00:08:53,799 --> 00:08:55,001
There's one among our chefs too.

236
00:08:55,067 --> 00:08:57,169
-I guess all Sons love comedy.
-They do.

237
00:08:57,236 --> 00:08:59,171
Yes, you're right!

238
00:08:59,238 --> 00:09:01,507
-He tries hard too.
-What's the psychology behind that?

239
00:09:01,574 --> 00:09:04,076
It was the kind of pleasure
I've never felt before.

240
00:09:05,378 --> 00:09:06,579
Do people laugh more now?

241
00:09:06,646 --> 00:09:08,881
-Not really.
-Here's the thing.

242
00:09:08,948 --> 00:09:11,884
-He should feel that frustration.
-Right.

243
00:09:11,951 --> 00:09:14,020
-He's never felt that.
-The trapezius is different.

244
00:09:14,086 --> 00:09:17,557
Those who don't have to make people laugh
tend to slant down.

245
00:09:17,623 --> 00:09:20,192
Look at this guy.
His shoulders rise every time he talks.

246
00:09:20,259 --> 00:09:21,794
It's not that easy.

247
00:09:21,861 --> 00:09:23,829
But I have to make people laugh.

248
00:09:23,896 --> 00:09:26,365
He gets all kinds of knots
in his shoulders.

249
00:09:26,432 --> 00:09:29,502
-He has said his shoulders were stiff.
-I try that hard.

250
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:30,736
How old is your son now?

251
00:09:30,803 --> 00:09:33,773
He's about to turn four.

252
00:09:34,006 --> 00:09:35,141
SAME AS POONG'S SON

253
00:09:35,207 --> 00:09:37,076
How do you make your four-year-old laugh?

254
00:09:37,143 --> 00:09:39,512
I don't talk to him like this,

255
00:09:39,579 --> 00:09:41,881
but I keep changing my character.

256
00:09:42,248 --> 00:09:44,050
YE-JIN'S ONE-PERSON SKIT

257
00:09:44,116 --> 00:09:46,819
I turn into different roles
and characters.

258
00:09:46,886 --> 00:09:48,054
Something like that.

259
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:49,221
Does his dad read to him too?

260
00:09:49,288 --> 00:09:50,523
Yes, at times.

261
00:09:50,590 --> 00:09:52,558
Their son is getting
high-quality reading time.

262
00:09:52,625 --> 00:09:54,594
-Every day before he sleeps.
-So high-quality.

263
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:55,661
It must be.

264
00:09:55,728 --> 00:09:58,230
Hyun-bin and Son Ye-jin

265
00:09:58,297 --> 00:10:01,467
act out the characters
as they read children's books.

266
00:10:01,534 --> 00:10:03,603
What if your son wants to be an actor?

267
00:10:04,637 --> 00:10:05,571
SIGH

268
00:10:06,238 --> 00:10:08,207
This may be the mom in me speaking,

269
00:10:08,274 --> 00:10:11,377
but I always think, "It'd be hard for him
not to be an actor with his looks."

270
00:10:11,711 --> 00:10:13,846
WOW

271
00:10:13,913 --> 00:10:15,314
HYUN-BIN, SON YE-JIN

272
00:10:15,381 --> 00:10:16,949
I mean, look at his parents.

273
00:10:17,016 --> 00:10:18,551
His DNA is different.

274
00:10:18,618 --> 00:10:21,721
I'm sorry. I've been told that
I shouldn't brag about my son.

275
00:10:21,787 --> 00:10:23,756
I should pay first before doing that.

276
00:10:23,823 --> 00:10:25,391
Who do you think he took after?

277
00:10:25,458 --> 00:10:27,093
He looks more like me.

278
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:29,495
Then it's game over.

279
00:10:29,562 --> 00:10:31,397
We both want him to take after each of us.

280
00:10:31,464 --> 00:10:33,199
-I see.
-So?

281
00:10:33,265 --> 00:10:35,101
What was it like to raise him

282
00:10:35,167 --> 00:10:37,803
-while filming <i>The Scandal?</i>
-It must've been rough.

283
00:10:37,870 --> 00:10:39,271
It seriously is not something

284
00:10:39,338 --> 00:10:41,641
-that's easy to do.
-No.

285
00:10:41,707 --> 00:10:44,977
Child-rearing is much harder than working.

286
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:47,146
I preferred going to the set.

287
00:10:47,213 --> 00:10:50,750
Right? You get to work
with people you can actually talk to.

288
00:10:50,816 --> 00:10:52,318
-And there's no need for repetition.
-No.

289
00:10:52,385 --> 00:10:54,654
I keep having
to put out the fire at home.

290
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:56,522
He wants to keep playing fire engine.

291
00:10:56,589 --> 00:10:58,624
So we have imaginary fires
going off all the time.

292
00:10:59,592 --> 00:11:01,394
-"It's a fire."
-We have to put it out.

293
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:03,929
-He keeps asking for help.
-And wailing the sirens?

294
00:11:04,530 --> 00:11:06,399
Here's another amazing fact.

295
00:11:06,465 --> 00:11:10,503
That Ye-jin is quite the cook.

296
00:11:10,569 --> 00:11:12,571
-I'm not.
-She's an excellent cook.

297
00:11:12,638 --> 00:11:13,739
I hear you're really good.

298
00:11:13,806 --> 00:11:16,409
-We prepared some pictures.
-That's awesome.

299
00:11:16,475 --> 00:11:18,244
Let's take a look at the photos.

300
00:11:20,046 --> 00:11:21,714
-Whoa.
-Whoa.

301
00:11:23,049 --> 00:11:24,316
It looks amazing sorted like this.

302
00:11:25,618 --> 00:11:27,820
-If you look…
-Is that pizza?

303
00:11:27,887 --> 00:11:29,422
And pasta.

304
00:11:29,488 --> 00:11:32,024
-Let's start with this one.
-Dumplings?

305
00:11:32,091 --> 00:11:33,959
Whoa. Look at how pretty they are.

306
00:11:34,026 --> 00:11:36,028
That was the first time I made dumplings.

307
00:11:36,095 --> 00:11:38,497
I bought the skin, though.

308
00:11:38,564 --> 00:11:39,632
-You did?
-They still look good.

309
00:11:39,699 --> 00:11:41,767
-It was fun.
-I see.

310
00:11:41,834 --> 00:11:43,569
-What about this?
-That one?

311
00:11:43,636 --> 00:11:45,304
It's fried chicken in soy sauce.

312
00:11:45,371 --> 00:11:48,474
-The teriyaki sauce?
-Soy sauce-based.

313
00:11:48,541 --> 00:11:50,576
-Look at the shredded green onion.
-And green onion.

314
00:11:50,643 --> 00:11:51,677
QUITE THIN

315
00:11:51,744 --> 00:11:53,446
-You shredded them like that?
-And this?

316
00:11:53,512 --> 00:11:56,615
The ones at the bottom
are mostly my kid's food.

317
00:11:56,682 --> 00:11:58,050
<i>-The bottom row?</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

318
00:11:58,117 --> 00:12:00,820
<i>-Except for that.</i>
<i>-These dishes? I see.</i>

319
00:12:00,886 --> 00:12:02,254
-I see his hands.
-And wait.

320
00:12:02,321 --> 00:12:04,590
<i>Here, and here.</i>

321
00:12:04,657 --> 00:12:07,226
<i>-Even his hands are pretty.</i>
<i>-He's waiting to eat.</i>

322
00:12:07,293 --> 00:12:09,462
Which one did Hyun-bin like best?

323
00:12:09,528 --> 00:12:10,730
The one up there,

324
00:12:10,796 --> 00:12:13,399
because he likes
dishes such as <i>kimchi-jjigae.</i>

325
00:12:13,466 --> 00:12:16,502
-He likes a meal like that.
-That's the perfect combo.

326
00:12:16,569 --> 00:12:19,038
-Steamed eggs and <i>kimchi-jjigae.</i>
-Steamed eggs and <i>kimchi-jjigae</i>?

327
00:12:19,105 --> 00:12:21,674
-And some roasted seaweed.
-I see.

328
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:23,142
What's this one?

329
00:12:23,209 --> 00:12:24,577
Sandfish <i>jjigae.</i>

330
00:12:24,643 --> 00:12:26,212
Whoa. You eat sandfish?

331
00:12:26,278 --> 00:12:27,680
It's my favorite fish.

332
00:12:27,747 --> 00:12:29,215
-Really?
-It sounds good.

333
00:12:29,281 --> 00:12:32,017
I love sand eel and sandfish.

334
00:12:32,084 --> 00:12:34,086
-That's so good.
-It pops nicely in your mouth.

335
00:12:34,153 --> 00:12:38,557
What's your go-to dish
when you have to make something good?

336
00:12:38,624 --> 00:12:40,493
Actually, there's this <i>gimbap</i>

337
00:12:40,559 --> 00:12:44,296
my late grandmother always made for me.

338
00:12:44,363 --> 00:12:45,364
So I eat that often

339
00:12:45,431 --> 00:12:49,034
and serve it to my guests,
and they love it too.

340
00:12:49,101 --> 00:12:51,604
-What's in it?
-How do you make it?

341
00:12:51,670 --> 00:12:55,941
Use beef strip loin or sirloin,
then marinate it.

342
00:12:56,008 --> 00:12:58,110
Red pepper powder, garlic,
cooking wine, plum <i>cheong.</i>

343
00:12:58,177 --> 00:13:00,813
And the point
is to use homemade soy sauce.

344
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:04,116
Then cook it
until there's a bit of liquid left.

345
00:13:04,183 --> 00:13:06,085
Add quite a lot of egg garnish

346
00:13:06,152 --> 00:13:10,156
and season pickled radish
with red pepper powder and vinegar.

347
00:13:10,222 --> 00:13:11,991
Those three ingredients are it.

348
00:13:12,057 --> 00:13:14,827
-You don't have to season the rice.
-Beef, pickled radish, egg garnish.

349
00:13:14,894 --> 00:13:16,629
That <i>gimbap </i>is really good.

350
00:13:16,695 --> 00:13:18,097
And you prepared some today?

351
00:13:18,464 --> 00:13:19,598
OH, MY…

352
00:13:19,665 --> 00:13:22,301
I literally thought about it yesterday.

353
00:13:22,368 --> 00:13:23,702
You did?

354
00:13:23,769 --> 00:13:26,105
But I only thought about it. I'm sorry.

355
00:13:26,172 --> 00:13:29,341
There are so many people here.

356
00:13:29,408 --> 00:13:32,211
I didn't think I could make
enough for everyone.

357
00:13:32,278 --> 00:13:35,347
I mean, I should make
at least one roll per person.

358
00:13:35,414 --> 00:13:37,883
-It's the thought that counts.
-If I get to return someday…

359
00:13:37,950 --> 00:13:39,218
Sure.

360
00:13:39,285 --> 00:13:41,353
Someday, dot dot dot.

361
00:13:41,687 --> 00:13:42,588
WE'LL WAIT FOR THAT DAY

362
00:13:42,655 --> 00:13:44,390
-We'll be waiting.
-Yes.

363
00:13:44,456 --> 00:13:46,525
She does seem like a good cook.

364
00:13:46,592 --> 00:13:49,662
I wonder what she keeps in her fridge.

365
00:13:49,728 --> 00:13:51,797
I'm getting worried.

366
00:13:51,864 --> 00:13:54,300
The chefs all have high expectations.

367
00:13:54,366 --> 00:13:56,502
It's the fridge
of the nation's first love.

368
00:13:56,569 --> 00:13:59,205
The thing is, she brought everything here.

369
00:13:59,271 --> 00:14:01,340
-What are the dad and the son eating?
-Right.

370
00:14:01,407 --> 00:14:02,775
They're with my mother-in-law.

371
00:14:04,643 --> 00:14:05,544
My mother-in-law.

372
00:14:05,611 --> 00:14:06,946
Is she also a good cook?

373
00:14:07,012 --> 00:14:09,615
Yes, she's really good,

374
00:14:09,682 --> 00:14:11,450
and I often receive lots of help.

375
00:14:11,517 --> 00:14:14,887
-Are we going to see her food in there?
-Yes.

376
00:14:14,954 --> 00:14:16,188
Thank you, Mother.

377
00:14:17,690 --> 00:14:19,058
-Now, let's see.
-Let's see it.

378
00:14:19,124 --> 00:14:20,226
I wonder what's in it.

379
00:14:20,292 --> 00:14:21,794
Why are you smiling so much?

380
00:14:22,661 --> 00:14:25,397
I'm just too curious.

381
00:14:25,464 --> 00:14:28,367
-I wonder what Hyun-bin likes to eat too.
-Right.

382
00:14:28,434 --> 00:14:30,202
-And she's a good cook.
-She is.

383
00:14:30,269 --> 00:14:32,905
-You saw the pictures.
-There's a lot of pressure.

384
00:14:32,972 --> 00:14:34,573
-So much pressure.
-The nation's first love.

385
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:36,709
-We present Ye-jin's fridge!
-Fridge!

386
00:14:37,443 --> 00:14:38,544
-Fridge!
-Fridge!

387
00:14:39,111 --> 00:14:41,680
AMAZED

388
00:14:41,747 --> 00:14:42,781
-Fridge!
-Fridge!

389
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:45,985
-That's a lot.
-It's neat.

390
00:14:46,051 --> 00:14:48,454
-She has lots of stuff.
-This family definitely cooks a lot.

391
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:49,688
It looks neat.

392
00:14:49,755 --> 00:14:51,323
I see some side dishes,

393
00:14:51,390 --> 00:14:53,292
as well as many sauces.

394
00:14:53,359 --> 00:14:55,594
Let's start from the top shelf.

395
00:14:55,661 --> 00:14:58,063
I see some dairy products, such as milk.

396
00:14:58,130 --> 00:15:00,733
-This is cheese, burrata. And yogurt.
-Burrata?

397
00:15:00,799 --> 00:15:02,701
We often eat it for breakfast.

398
00:15:02,768 --> 00:15:05,004
-Do you eat a light breakfast?
-Yes.

399
00:15:05,070 --> 00:15:07,172
I have to eat a light breakfast
to look pretty.

400
00:15:07,239 --> 00:15:09,408
-What's your breakfast?
-<i>Ramyeon.</i>

401
00:15:09,475 --> 00:15:10,676
Come on.

402
00:15:10,743 --> 00:15:11,777
Stop lying.

403
00:15:11,844 --> 00:15:13,579
I almost had another one earlier.

404
00:15:13,646 --> 00:15:15,648
There's this bread
I really want to recommend.

405
00:15:16,749 --> 00:15:18,217
-Bread?
-It must be in the back.

406
00:15:18,284 --> 00:15:20,119
-It's here.
-It was in the back.

407
00:15:20,185 --> 00:15:22,087
Sunchoke French sourdough?

408
00:15:22,154 --> 00:15:24,456
I bought it online by chance

409
00:15:24,523 --> 00:15:26,492
-and it was so good.
-What is it?

410
00:15:26,558 --> 00:15:28,527
-It's really nice.
-I need that to look prettier.

411
00:15:28,594 --> 00:15:30,095
I'll get it right away.

412
00:15:30,162 --> 00:15:31,964
-Right now.
-Sunchoke… I'll buy it too.

413
00:15:32,031 --> 00:15:34,066
I need to buy it now. Where's my phone?

414
00:15:34,133 --> 00:15:35,501
Is this it?

415
00:15:35,567 --> 00:15:37,236
-That's it.
-I see.

416
00:15:37,303 --> 00:15:38,404
Which one?

417
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:41,073
-Which one, Poong?
-We love recommendations.

418
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:44,209
If someone says something's good,
we all buy it.

419
00:15:44,276 --> 00:15:45,644
Which one? I can't find it.

420
00:15:46,078 --> 00:15:48,948
EVERYONE'S BUSY BUYING

421
00:15:49,014 --> 00:15:51,216
-Only three left.
-It's harder than I thought.

422
00:15:51,617 --> 00:15:53,452
SHE FINALLY BOUGHT IT TOO

423
00:15:53,519 --> 00:15:55,387
How do you eat it in the morning?

424
00:15:55,454 --> 00:15:57,489
I just toast it lightly

425
00:15:57,556 --> 00:16:00,359
and eat it with butter,
or with olive oil and balsamic vinegar.

426
00:16:00,426 --> 00:16:02,027
It has a nice texture,

427
00:16:02,094 --> 00:16:04,196
and it tastes quite nutty.

428
00:16:04,263 --> 00:16:07,266
What's your son's favorite breakfast?

429
00:16:07,333 --> 00:16:12,738
He loves oatmeal, sweet pumpkin,
and corn bread.

430
00:16:13,305 --> 00:16:14,640
He likes healthy food.

431
00:16:14,707 --> 00:16:17,910
I mix oatmeal with milk and egg,

432
00:16:17,977 --> 00:16:21,213
slice in sweet pumpkin, corn, and apple,

433
00:16:21,280 --> 00:16:23,415
then bake it in the air fryer.

434
00:16:24,850 --> 00:16:26,452
He actually likes to eat

435
00:16:26,518 --> 00:16:28,487
things such as <i>jeon,</i> fritters.

436
00:16:28,554 --> 00:16:30,456
-He likes <i>jeon</i>?
-And <i>tteok,</i> rice cake.

437
00:16:30,522 --> 00:16:31,623
And <i>tteok</i>?

438
00:16:31,690 --> 00:16:35,227
-He has a pretty great appetite.
-What a relief.

439
00:16:35,294 --> 00:16:36,729
And he never asks for candy?

440
00:16:36,795 --> 00:16:38,197
He does, but when he can't have it,

441
00:16:38,263 --> 00:16:40,265
he just accepts that.

442
00:16:40,332 --> 00:16:41,834
He's the stable type.

443
00:16:41,900 --> 00:16:43,502
When I did that with my son,

444
00:16:43,569 --> 00:16:44,870
he sprawled out on the ground.

445
00:16:44,937 --> 00:16:48,440
We're raising him very strictly.

446
00:16:48,507 --> 00:16:50,242
He gets a time-out if he does that.

447
00:16:50,309 --> 00:16:51,944
You can't give in.

448
00:16:52,011 --> 00:16:54,446
Does he eat more like his dad or you?

449
00:16:54,513 --> 00:16:57,116
I think he's more like his dad.

450
00:16:57,182 --> 00:16:58,984
He likes mild flavors.

451
00:16:59,051 --> 00:17:00,853
Does his dad also like subtle flavors?

452
00:17:00,919 --> 00:17:02,921
He eats <i>seolleongtang,</i> ox bone soup,

453
00:17:02,988 --> 00:17:04,156
without adding salt.

454
00:17:04,223 --> 00:17:05,624
NO SALT

455
00:17:05,691 --> 00:17:06,892
Mild soup, mild flavor.

456
00:17:06,959 --> 00:17:10,329
He seems to enjoy that mild soup flavor.

457
00:17:10,396 --> 00:17:12,331
I have a really bad sweet tooth.

458
00:17:12,398 --> 00:17:15,534
The couple has different tastes in food.

459
00:17:15,601 --> 00:17:18,404
Whose appetite do you two lean towards?

460
00:17:18,470 --> 00:17:21,473
I'm the cook,
so everything is made to my taste.

461
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:24,943
-"Eat what you're given."
-Exactly. Eat what you're given.

462
00:17:25,010 --> 00:17:26,979
It must be great when your son eats well,

463
00:17:27,046 --> 00:17:28,914
but at times, he might leave leftovers.

464
00:17:28,981 --> 00:17:30,449
Yes, and I finish it all.

465
00:17:30,516 --> 00:17:31,583
Yes, of course.

466
00:17:31,650 --> 00:17:32,885
-Right? It's a waste.
-Right.

467
00:17:32,951 --> 00:17:35,220
The mom eats the kid's leftovers,

468
00:17:35,287 --> 00:17:37,556
then when she can't finish it,
it goes to the dad.

469
00:17:38,257 --> 00:17:40,859
-Correct. The unwritten rule.
-That's how it is.

470
00:17:40,926 --> 00:17:43,796
When we're eating something good,
I hope my son leaves leftovers.

471
00:17:44,696 --> 00:17:45,864
Yes.

472
00:17:45,931 --> 00:17:48,934
I'd think, "He won't eat it all, right?"

473
00:17:49,001 --> 00:17:51,470
-I can't say it out loud, though.
-How unfortunate.

474
00:17:51,537 --> 00:17:52,971
-Dads are like that.
-Yes.

475
00:17:53,038 --> 00:17:56,542
-The second shelf. This is it.
-Side dishes.

476
00:17:56,608 --> 00:17:59,645
There are so many side dishes
on the second shelf.

477
00:17:59,711 --> 00:18:02,815
Basil pesto, tomato marinade,

478
00:18:02,881 --> 00:18:04,917
-and cold cucumber soup.
-Cucumber soup.

479
00:18:04,983 --> 00:18:06,185
They're on this side.

480
00:18:06,251 --> 00:18:08,687
She must've made these.

481
00:18:08,754 --> 00:18:10,489
-Is that so?
-Yes.

482
00:18:10,556 --> 00:18:13,392
-She's good.
-She made all these.

483
00:18:13,459 --> 00:18:15,461
What about those side dishes?

484
00:18:15,527 --> 00:18:17,863
Those are all from my mother-in-law.

485
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:18,997
-These?
-Yes.

486
00:18:19,064 --> 00:18:22,034
-Such as seasoned dried pollack fillet.
-Dried pollack fillet.

487
00:18:22,101 --> 00:18:23,902
-Let's do it.
-I can't just sit here.

488
00:18:24,470 --> 00:18:26,638
-Let's ask for a recommendation.
-Pick one.

489
00:18:26,705 --> 00:18:28,874
We should try at least one of these.

490
00:18:28,941 --> 00:18:32,811
-Try the soup, this, and this.
-This and this?

491
00:18:32,878 --> 00:18:34,113
I'll open it.

492
00:18:34,179 --> 00:18:35,747
FIRST, THE TOMATO MARINADE

493
00:18:36,482 --> 00:18:38,484
-People actually make that at home?
-Right?

494
00:18:38,550 --> 00:18:41,086
I didn't use any salt
to eat it with my son.

495
00:18:41,153 --> 00:18:44,223
-Cold cucumber soup.
-I'm dying to try this one.

496
00:18:44,289 --> 00:18:47,793
I made sure it isn't too salty
for my son to have it.

497
00:18:47,860 --> 00:18:49,561
It happens when you have a kid.

498
00:18:49,628 --> 00:18:51,396
-Okay, then. Chefs?
-Chefs?

499
00:18:51,463 --> 00:18:54,766
-What? No way.
-Try whichever one you like.

500
00:18:54,833 --> 00:18:56,602
-This is pesto.
-All of you?

501
00:18:56,668 --> 00:18:58,470
-Yes, these.
-Really?

502
00:18:58,837 --> 00:19:01,206
SWARMING

503
00:19:01,273 --> 00:19:04,009
-Let's see how good she is.
-She peeled the tomatoes.

504
00:19:04,076 --> 00:19:07,546
-You can analyze everything.
-Wait, this is embarrassing.

505
00:19:07,613 --> 00:19:10,516
It's funnier to see them
lined up to try her food.

506
00:19:10,582 --> 00:19:13,118
You can go around
and tell people about it.

507
00:19:13,185 --> 00:19:16,321
If I had known,
I would've tried even harder.

508
00:19:16,388 --> 00:19:18,790
-Don't mind them.
-We have to cook with it later,

509
00:19:18,857 --> 00:19:21,360
-…so we must try them.
-Look at this.

510
00:19:21,426 --> 00:19:22,528
Can I have a bit?

511
00:19:23,128 --> 00:19:25,664
-You aren't at a restaurant.
-Just see how she likes her food.

512
00:19:25,731 --> 00:19:27,065
TRYING THE COLD CUCUMBER SOUP

513
00:19:27,699 --> 00:19:28,767
I see.

514
00:19:29,501 --> 00:19:30,736
NODDING

515
00:19:30,802 --> 00:19:33,205
-It's mild, isn't it?
-It is.

516
00:19:33,272 --> 00:19:35,174
It's made perfectly for a child.

517
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:38,510
At times, you end up
choking on the cold soup

518
00:19:38,577 --> 00:19:39,745
-because of the sourness.
-Yes.

519
00:19:39,811 --> 00:19:41,346
But this was perfectly sour.

520
00:19:41,413 --> 00:19:43,782
-Really? Thank you.
-Everything's good.

521
00:19:43,849 --> 00:19:45,083
Does your son like this?

522
00:19:45,150 --> 00:19:46,385
-Does he eat it too?
-He does.

523
00:19:46,451 --> 00:19:49,188
He loves white kimchi and <i>dongchimi.</i>

524
00:19:49,254 --> 00:19:51,123
He's into Korean cuisine.

525
00:19:51,190 --> 00:19:54,993
My kid devours them
and even drinks the soup.

526
00:19:55,194 --> 00:19:56,562
TOMATO MARINADE

527
00:19:56,628 --> 00:20:00,399
-With some vinegar…
-This basil pesto is great too.

528
00:20:00,465 --> 00:20:02,434
-It's great.
-She peeled the tomatoes.

529
00:20:02,501 --> 00:20:05,737
-The tomatoes?
-It's easier to eat without the peel.

530
00:20:06,405 --> 00:20:07,406
The pesto is so good.

531
00:20:08,574 --> 00:20:10,142
The basil pesto is so good.

532
00:20:10,209 --> 00:20:12,144
-You could even sell it.
-Right?

533
00:20:12,211 --> 00:20:13,579
The texture of the sauce

534
00:20:13,645 --> 00:20:16,215
is perfect.

535
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:19,318
It ends up too runny
when you blend it for too long.

536
00:20:19,384 --> 00:20:20,552
The texture's great.

537
00:20:20,619 --> 00:20:22,020
I'm flattered.

538
00:20:22,087 --> 00:20:24,223
You've done a great job
at bringing out the freshness.

539
00:20:24,289 --> 00:20:25,924
Have you tried it, Jong-won?

540
00:20:25,991 --> 00:20:28,894
-Did you measure everything?
-I didn't.

541
00:20:29,561 --> 00:20:31,129
Son Ye-jin must be
good with her <i>son,</i> hands.

542
00:20:31,196 --> 00:20:32,564
READING HIS FACE

543
00:20:32,631 --> 00:20:34,366
So you eyeball it?

544
00:20:34,433 --> 00:20:36,702
HA HA HA HA HA

545
00:20:37,135 --> 00:20:39,071
ON EPISODE 83,
WE RECEIVED AN ANONYMOUS COMPLAINT

546
00:20:39,137 --> 00:20:42,140
IT WAS FROM JONG-WON,
WHO WANTED TO MAKE PEOPLE LAUGH

547
00:20:42,674 --> 00:20:44,743
SOLUTION: HE'LL KEEP LAUGHING

548
00:20:45,143 --> 00:20:46,979
A MONTH INTO JONG-WON'S SOLUTION

549
00:20:47,246 --> 00:20:48,080
CONFIDENCE +1

550
00:20:49,448 --> 00:20:51,850
HA HA HA HA HA

551
00:20:51,917 --> 00:20:53,018
He's been funny lately.

552
00:20:54,319 --> 00:20:55,254
The next shelf.

553
00:20:55,320 --> 00:20:56,955
I see lots of meat here.

554
00:20:57,022 --> 00:20:59,391
Beef and chicken.

555
00:20:59,458 --> 00:21:04,363
I heard children
should eat meat at least once a day.

556
00:21:04,429 --> 00:21:05,464
So I switch between them.

557
00:21:05,530 --> 00:21:07,332
In case he gets sick of it.

558
00:21:07,399 --> 00:21:10,269
People usually keep meat in the freezer.

559
00:21:10,335 --> 00:21:12,271
-Do you use it right away?
-Yes, I use it up right away.

560
00:21:12,337 --> 00:21:14,873
When I end up freezing the meat,
it's for my husband and me.

561
00:21:16,275 --> 00:21:18,277
What's your favorite kind of meat?

562
00:21:18,343 --> 00:21:21,580
-Pork belly is my favorite.
-Pork belly.

563
00:21:21,647 --> 00:21:23,548
And down another shelf.

564
00:21:23,615 --> 00:21:25,183
Fried tofu pocket.

565
00:21:25,250 --> 00:21:27,052
That's for our kid,

566
00:21:27,119 --> 00:21:28,654
and we use it to lose weight

567
00:21:28,720 --> 00:21:30,555
by adding tofu inside, instead of rice.

568
00:21:31,390 --> 00:21:35,627
I squeeze the water out of tofu
and mix it with burdock and stuff it in.

569
00:21:35,694 --> 00:21:37,729
-That's a good recipe.
-When we go on a diet.

570
00:21:37,796 --> 00:21:41,366
-You must like that crunchy texture.
-I do.

571
00:21:41,433 --> 00:21:43,368
Are you still on a diet? What about today?

572
00:21:43,435 --> 00:21:44,970
Well, it's over, anyway.

573
00:21:45,037 --> 00:21:46,238
I had Chinese food yesterday.

574
00:21:46,305 --> 00:21:47,839
A CHINESE FOOD PARTY

575
00:21:47,906 --> 00:21:49,308
Sweet and sour pork,

576
00:21:49,374 --> 00:21:50,609
stir-fried eggplant,

577
00:21:50,676 --> 00:21:51,510
dumplings,

578
00:21:51,576 --> 00:21:53,345
<i>jjajangmyeon,</i> and even <i>jjamppong.</i>

579
00:21:53,412 --> 00:21:56,148
-It must've been good.
-It was.

580
00:21:56,214 --> 00:21:58,150
So it's over, anyway.

581
00:21:59,318 --> 00:22:01,253
-Please make me lots of good food.
-Okay.

582
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:02,888
-She rekindled her appetite yesterday.
-Yes.

583
00:22:02,954 --> 00:22:04,523
Now, the crisper drawer.

584
00:22:04,589 --> 00:22:06,725
-Carrots.
-Carrots, eggplants, and burdock.

585
00:22:06,792 --> 00:22:09,261
-This is burdock.
-Burdock.

586
00:22:09,328 --> 00:22:10,829
Is there a particular favorite?

587
00:22:10,896 --> 00:22:13,498
-I love eggplants so much.
-Eggplants.

588
00:22:13,565 --> 00:22:15,801
-I never ate it as a kid, though.
-Right.

589
00:22:15,867 --> 00:22:18,036
-Not many do.
-I think it comes with age.

590
00:22:18,103 --> 00:22:20,605
But now I'm addicted.
I had it yesterday too.

591
00:22:20,672 --> 00:22:21,540
Right.

592
00:22:21,606 --> 00:22:23,475
I love eggplants so much

593
00:22:23,542 --> 00:22:27,245
that I sometimes make <i>disanxian</i>,
eggplant, potato, and bell peppers.

594
00:22:27,312 --> 00:22:30,048
It takes a lot of work, but it's so good.

595
00:22:30,115 --> 00:22:32,484
-It is good.
-It's not easy to make at home.

596
00:22:32,551 --> 00:22:35,587
-There's an eggplant in here too.
-There's another one.

597
00:22:35,654 --> 00:22:37,022
My aesthetician keeps a garden

598
00:22:37,089 --> 00:22:39,524
and grows organic vegetables.

599
00:22:39,591 --> 00:22:41,893
-I see.
-There's a cute little watermelon too.

600
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:42,894
-They grew these?
-Yes.

601
00:22:42,961 --> 00:22:44,463
You'll see yellow eggplants too.

602
00:22:44,529 --> 00:22:46,865
-These are eggplants?
-Those.

603
00:22:46,932 --> 00:22:48,734
-They are.
-They look like one.

604
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:50,869
Do you now get why it's called "eggplant"?

605
00:22:50,936 --> 00:22:54,039
-Right. It looks like an egg.
-It's "eggplant" in English.

606
00:22:54,106 --> 00:22:55,907
It's originally that round.

607
00:22:55,974 --> 00:22:58,677
What's this about a misunderstanding
caused by organic veggies?

608
00:22:58,744 --> 00:23:02,280
They were so cute,
so I uploaded them on my social media,

609
00:23:02,347 --> 00:23:04,549
and people thought I grew those.

610
00:23:04,616 --> 00:23:05,817
SON YE-JIN GROWS ORGANIC VEGGIES

611
00:23:05,884 --> 00:23:08,286
It said,
"She cares this much about her son."

612
00:23:08,353 --> 00:23:11,757
So I had to explain myself
and say that I got it as a present.

613
00:23:12,491 --> 00:23:14,726
We just received another story.

614
00:23:14,793 --> 00:23:18,196
A picture of a dish you made

615
00:23:18,263 --> 00:23:20,565
became a huge issue?

616
00:23:20,632 --> 00:23:23,001
-What is it?
-Let us show you the picture.

617
00:23:23,335 --> 00:23:25,036
THE PICTURE

618
00:23:25,103 --> 00:23:26,238
This is it.

619
00:23:26,304 --> 00:23:29,074
This caused quite a controversy.

620
00:23:29,141 --> 00:23:31,276
And many people commented
their take on it.

621
00:23:31,343 --> 00:23:32,310
Is this soup

622
00:23:32,377 --> 00:23:35,647
yukgaejang,<i> spicy beef soup,</i>
<i>or </i>soegogi-muguk,<i> beef and radish soup?</i>

623
00:23:35,714 --> 00:23:37,149
-A-ha.
-<i>Yukgaejang</i>?

624
00:23:37,215 --> 00:23:38,717
Those on team <i>yukgaejang</i> said,

625
00:23:38,784 --> 00:23:41,386
"There are too many ingredients
to call it <i>soegogi-muguk</i>."

626
00:23:41,453 --> 00:23:42,754
-"So it's <i>yukgaejang."</i>
-There's no radish.

627
00:23:42,821 --> 00:23:43,755
While others said,

628
00:23:43,822 --> 00:23:46,525
"No, you cut the beef in that shape
to make <i>soegogi-muguk</i>."

629
00:23:46,591 --> 00:23:47,426
Wait.

630
00:23:47,492 --> 00:23:50,061
"No, there's bracken, so it's <i>yukgaejang</i>."

631
00:23:50,128 --> 00:23:51,196
It's <i>yukgaejang.</i>

632
00:23:51,263 --> 00:23:54,332
"No, in Daegu,
we use bracken in our <i>soegogi-muguk</i>."

633
00:23:54,399 --> 00:23:55,867
It's Daegu-style.

634
00:23:55,934 --> 00:23:58,036
Some are still fighting over it.

635
00:23:58,103 --> 00:24:00,272
So was that <i>yukgaejang </i>or <i>soegogi-muguk?</i>

636
00:24:00,338 --> 00:24:02,641
-<i>Yukgaejang.</i>
-It's hard to tell.

637
00:24:02,707 --> 00:24:05,811
-But I don't see taro stems.
-Yes, it's missing taro.

638
00:24:05,877 --> 00:24:08,847
-Taro stems.
-Yes, taro stems.

639
00:24:08,914 --> 00:24:10,782
Will you put an end to this?

640
00:24:10,849 --> 00:24:13,285
Was that <i>yukgaejang </i>or <i>soegogi-muguk</i>?

641
00:24:13,351 --> 00:24:14,519
<i>Soegogi-muguk.</i>

642
00:24:14,586 --> 00:24:16,121
WHAT COULD THE FINAL VERDICT BE?

643
00:24:16,855 --> 00:24:17,823
It was <i>yukgaejang.</i>

644
00:24:17,889 --> 00:24:20,592
-See? It's <i>yukgaejang.</i>
-That's <i>yukgaejang</i>?

645
00:24:20,659 --> 00:24:23,028
I think it was the beef.

646
00:24:23,094 --> 00:24:26,298
-It's usually ripped into pieces, right?
-Yes.

647
00:24:26,364 --> 00:24:28,233
-But I couldn't be bothered.
-So you chopped it.

648
00:24:28,300 --> 00:24:30,769
Yes, so I just chopped it up.

649
00:24:30,836 --> 00:24:33,405
And then I saw many were debating over it.

650
00:24:33,472 --> 00:24:35,073
The controversy has been resolved.

651
00:24:35,140 --> 00:24:36,708
Isn't it just the three of you at home?

652
00:24:36,775 --> 00:24:39,711
-But you made a lot.
-It's a lot.

653
00:24:39,778 --> 00:24:43,215
As you know, for soup,
the bigger the batch, the better it is.

654
00:24:43,281 --> 00:24:46,318
I had guests come over,
so I gave it out to them later.

655
00:24:46,384 --> 00:24:48,119
I see. Now, the door shelf.

656
00:24:48,186 --> 00:24:50,322
She has many kinds of soy sauce.

657
00:24:50,388 --> 00:24:52,858
-Flavored soy sauce.
-Low-sugar flavored soy sauce.

658
00:24:52,924 --> 00:24:54,459
How many are there?

659
00:24:54,526 --> 00:24:56,795
People who use
different kinds of soy sauce

660
00:24:56,862 --> 00:24:58,763
-are always good cooks.
-Right.

661
00:24:58,830 --> 00:24:59,831
Is this correct?

662
00:24:59,898 --> 00:25:01,399
I think he's completely right.

663
00:25:02,601 --> 00:25:06,571
So those who use different soy sauces
for different dishes are good cooks.

664
00:25:06,638 --> 00:25:08,306
Great job pointing that out.

665
00:25:08,373 --> 00:25:11,243
Excellent job. Awesome two cents.

666
00:25:11,309 --> 00:25:13,278
Good. She has all these sauces.

667
00:25:13,345 --> 00:25:15,013
-Sesame oil.
-What's this?

668
00:25:15,080 --> 00:25:17,682
-That's perilla oil.
-Perilla oil.

669
00:25:17,749 --> 00:25:18,717
Who is this from?

670
00:25:18,783 --> 00:25:21,219
-My mother-in-law pressed it at a mill.
-Herself?

671
00:25:21,286 --> 00:25:22,554
-Such a nice color.
-Right?

672
00:25:22,621 --> 00:25:24,523
What's this? Fish sauce?
<i>Chopi</i>-infused <i>aekjeot?</i>

673
00:25:24,589 --> 00:25:26,625
-Infused with prickly ash?
-It adds a nice touch.

674
00:25:26,691 --> 00:25:27,859
<i>Chopi</i>-infused <i>aekjeot</i>?

675
00:25:27,926 --> 00:25:29,594
-You've used quite a lot.
-Yes.

676
00:25:29,661 --> 00:25:32,998
A dash of it makes the dish even better.

677
00:25:33,064 --> 00:25:36,668
-Smell it.
-It must be one of your secrets.

678
00:25:36,735 --> 00:25:38,203
And right here,

679
00:25:38,270 --> 00:25:40,572
-facial masks.
-Facial masks.

680
00:25:40,639 --> 00:25:44,876
-People use these often.
-I keep them in there to cool my face.

681
00:25:44,943 --> 00:25:46,678
I have some in my fridge too.

682
00:25:46,745 --> 00:25:48,847
So this is her skincare routine.

683
00:25:48,914 --> 00:25:49,881
The freezer.

684
00:25:49,948 --> 00:25:53,351
<i>Tteok,</i> rice cake,
and <i>ongsimi</i>, sticky rice dumplings.

685
00:25:53,418 --> 00:25:56,121
I keep <i>tteok</i>
in bar shapes and chopped.

686
00:25:56,187 --> 00:25:57,556
You must like <i>tteok.</i>

687
00:25:57,622 --> 00:26:00,625
I could eat <i>tteokbokki</i> every day
if I had the chance.

688
00:26:00,692 --> 00:26:03,528
I literally did that in high school.

689
00:26:03,595 --> 00:26:04,796
-I see.
-Yes.

690
00:26:04,863 --> 00:26:06,364
I love <i>tteokbokki.</i>

691
00:26:06,431 --> 00:26:09,367
-Do you like all <i>bunsik,</i> street food?
-Yes, I do.

692
00:26:09,434 --> 00:26:10,569
I have a huge sweet tooth,

693
00:26:10,635 --> 00:26:14,406
-and I love everything that makes me fat.
-You do.

694
00:26:14,472 --> 00:26:16,341
She said she loved sweet things too.

695
00:26:16,408 --> 00:26:18,276
What could this be?

696
00:26:18,343 --> 00:26:20,979
-Hamburger patties that I made.
-Patties.

697
00:26:21,046 --> 00:26:22,314
Hamburger patties.

698
00:26:22,380 --> 00:26:24,482
-Made with beef and… Yes.
-You made it yourself?

699
00:26:24,549 --> 00:26:27,285
My son and husband both love burgers.

700
00:26:27,352 --> 00:26:30,922
When I made it
with ground strip loin and pork belly,

701
00:26:30,989 --> 00:26:32,557
it was so delicious.

702
00:26:32,624 --> 00:26:34,626
-But it takes too much work.
-Right.

703
00:26:34,693 --> 00:26:37,128
And I could never find ground pork belly,

704
00:26:37,195 --> 00:26:38,363
but I found a place.

705
00:26:38,430 --> 00:26:40,398
So I recently got that

706
00:26:40,465 --> 00:26:42,300
and made it with ground pork belly.

707
00:26:42,367 --> 00:26:43,935
-Is it already seasoned?
-Yes.

708
00:26:44,002 --> 00:26:46,705
-Oyster sauce, garlic, breadcrumbs…
-It smells like garlic.

709
00:26:46,771 --> 00:26:48,607
-…salt, and pepper.
-So it's not just ground meat.

710
00:26:48,673 --> 00:26:51,209
-I've seasoned it already.
-It's seasoned.

711
00:26:51,276 --> 00:26:53,244
And there is a pile of seafood.

712
00:26:53,311 --> 00:26:55,981
-Tilefish, dried yellow corvina, mackerel.
-Mackerel too.

713
00:26:56,047 --> 00:26:57,282
And cutlassfish.

714
00:26:57,349 --> 00:27:00,385
-Abalones too. Octopuses.
-Shrimp and octopuses.

715
00:27:00,452 --> 00:27:01,920
My kid loves them.

716
00:27:01,987 --> 00:27:04,322
Also, tilefish is delicious, right?

717
00:27:04,389 --> 00:27:06,758
-But it comes with so many bones.
-The bones.

718
00:27:06,825 --> 00:27:09,861
I debone everything with my hands
and take a second look,

719
00:27:09,928 --> 00:27:11,363
but he still finds bones with every bite.

720
00:27:11,429 --> 00:27:14,866
So I told him not to trust me
and pick them out himself.

721
00:27:14,933 --> 00:27:18,336
-That he had to spit it out.
-That's it.

722
00:27:18,403 --> 00:27:20,572
He said yes and kept spitting them out.

723
00:27:20,639 --> 00:27:22,941
-It keeps coming.
-You should use forceps.

724
00:27:23,008 --> 00:27:24,342
Shelf-stable ingredients.

725
00:27:24,409 --> 00:27:27,112
I thought this was a trophy. What's this?

726
00:27:27,178 --> 00:27:28,780
-This looks fancy.
-Actually…

727
00:27:28,847 --> 00:27:30,949
My husband got that honey as a gift,

728
00:27:31,016 --> 00:27:32,550
and it's apparently quite valuable.

729
00:27:32,617 --> 00:27:35,120
-It's honey?
-So I brought it in secret.

730
00:27:36,521 --> 00:27:38,123
I've never had it, though.

731
00:27:38,189 --> 00:27:40,625
But it looked cool,
and he said it was valuable.

732
00:27:40,692 --> 00:27:42,961
Can our chefs use this if they must?

733
00:27:43,028 --> 00:27:45,330
He can't get mad at me anyway.

734
00:27:46,598 --> 00:27:49,434
-That's true.
-Right.

735
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:50,635
Try it.

736
00:27:50,702 --> 00:27:52,270
-What?
-Can we?

737
00:27:52,337 --> 00:27:54,205
The lid is quite heavy.

738
00:27:54,272 --> 00:27:56,307
-I want to try it.
-You should pull it up.

739
00:27:57,542 --> 00:27:59,244
-You've had a lot.
-You have.

740
00:27:59,310 --> 00:28:01,179
So only Hyun-bin had this?

741
00:28:01,246 --> 00:28:02,347
Yes.

742
00:28:02,414 --> 00:28:04,215
-It must be good.
-Well…

743
00:28:04,282 --> 00:28:06,384
-I don't know if we should both taste it.
-Right?

744
00:28:06,451 --> 00:28:08,053
-I'll try it and tell you.
-Please.

745
00:28:08,119 --> 00:28:09,587
Let's do rock-paper-scissors.

746
00:28:10,355 --> 00:28:12,157
You should try it.

747
00:28:12,223 --> 00:28:14,426
The honey that Hyun-bin
had been eating in secret.

748
00:28:14,492 --> 00:28:17,228
The one he's eating in secret
without even sharing it with his wife.

749
00:28:17,295 --> 00:28:18,897
-Use a clean spoon.
-What is this?

750
00:28:18,963 --> 00:28:21,099
-Look at the color.
-The color's different.

751
00:28:21,166 --> 00:28:23,201
It's as dark as caramel.

752
00:28:23,601 --> 00:28:25,070
YUM

753
00:28:25,136 --> 00:28:26,538
-So?
-It must be savory.

754
00:28:27,639 --> 00:28:29,641
-This must be expensive.
-Really?

755
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:31,743
-Can you tell?
-It's different from ordinary honey.

756
00:28:31,810 --> 00:28:33,411
Try it. It's okay.

757
00:28:33,478 --> 00:28:35,613
SPRINGS UP IMMEDIATELY

758
00:28:35,680 --> 00:28:36,514
The money's there.

759
00:28:36,581 --> 00:28:39,050
I've never had it either,
so I'll try some too.

760
00:28:39,617 --> 00:28:41,886
This is Ye-jin's first time too.

761
00:28:41,953 --> 00:28:42,921
FIRST TIME TRYING IT

762
00:28:43,822 --> 00:28:45,223
-Whoa.
-Mmm.

763
00:28:45,790 --> 00:28:48,126
Hyun-bin will get mad when he sees this.

764
00:28:48,193 --> 00:28:51,162
Sorry, Hyun-bin.
I was going to try it alone.

765
00:28:51,229 --> 00:28:52,664
Don't let it drip.

766
00:28:52,731 --> 00:28:54,833
LICK

767
00:28:55,500 --> 00:28:57,802
-It smells amazing.
-It does.

768
00:28:57,869 --> 00:29:00,038
I feel much healthier.

769
00:29:00,105 --> 00:29:02,507
-My voice even sounds better.
-It must be a rare treat.

770
00:29:02,574 --> 00:29:04,375
Thanks a lot.

771
00:29:04,442 --> 00:29:05,510
You know,

772
00:29:05,577 --> 00:29:07,312
your wife got curious and had it.

773
00:29:07,378 --> 00:29:09,781
Please don't take it the wrong way.

774
00:29:09,848 --> 00:29:11,416
That was all for Ye-jin's fridge!

775
00:29:11,483 --> 00:29:13,485
FINISHED CHECKING OUT YE-JIN'S FRIDGE

776
00:29:13,551 --> 00:29:17,589
What kind of food
is she looking to eat today?

777
00:29:17,655 --> 00:29:20,158
Let's find out her orders.

778
00:29:20,225 --> 00:29:22,060
These are Ye-jin's orders.

779
00:29:22,127 --> 00:29:24,395
Someone Who Cooks Well for Her Guests.

780
00:29:24,462 --> 00:29:27,132
-I see.
-What do you mean by this?

781
00:29:27,198 --> 00:29:31,503
When I have guests over,
I want to use the ingredients I have

782
00:29:31,569 --> 00:29:34,472
-yet make something fancy.
-Fancy.

783
00:29:34,539 --> 00:29:39,377
So if you have a particular secret,
I want to learn that.

784
00:29:40,145 --> 00:29:42,947
A dish made with ordinary ingredients
that can put people in awe.

785
00:29:43,014 --> 00:29:45,350
Something that makes them go,
"How did you make this?"

786
00:29:45,416 --> 00:29:47,552
-Something they'd ask the recipe for.
-Yes.

787
00:29:47,619 --> 00:29:49,020
What's the second order?

788
00:29:49,687 --> 00:29:51,556
Summer Eggplant Scent.

789
00:29:51,623 --> 00:29:53,958
-She said she likes eggplants.
-Yes.

790
00:29:54,025 --> 00:29:56,094
-It's in season.
-She must want an eggplant dish.

791
00:29:56,161 --> 00:29:58,129
I've been wondering

792
00:29:58,196 --> 00:30:02,033
if there was a new eggplant dish
that I've never tried

793
00:30:02,100 --> 00:30:06,638
and thought the chefs
might have their own recipes.

794
00:30:06,704 --> 00:30:08,072
So I want to learn that too.

795
00:30:08,139 --> 00:30:10,975
Ye-jin is here alone today,

796
00:30:11,042 --> 00:30:13,611
while we have nine chefs.

797
00:30:13,678 --> 00:30:15,446
This means

798
00:30:15,513 --> 00:30:18,249
whoever calls first dibs is in.

799
00:30:18,316 --> 00:30:22,320
You can jump in on your own,
or even as a team.

800
00:30:22,387 --> 00:30:26,090
-Feel free to join in many ways.
-A team round too?

801
00:30:26,157 --> 00:30:28,526
You're free to join. Jong-won?

802
00:30:28,593 --> 00:30:30,495
The Sons.

803
00:30:31,663 --> 00:30:32,931
You should be joining.

804
00:30:32,997 --> 00:30:34,599
As a fellow Son,

805
00:30:34,666 --> 00:30:36,301
I'd like to cook for the beautiful lady

806
00:30:36,367 --> 00:30:37,502
and make a dish for her guests.

807
00:30:39,137 --> 00:30:40,271
All right.

808
00:30:40,338 --> 00:30:42,040
First-come, first-served.

809
00:30:42,106 --> 00:30:45,009
Sung-jun? Which one will it be? Eggplant?

810
00:30:45,076 --> 00:30:47,312
I'd like to challenge Chef Jong-won.

811
00:30:47,378 --> 00:30:50,014
So I'll join
Someone Who Cooks Well for Her Guests.

812
00:30:50,081 --> 00:30:53,651
Is that so?
Sung-jun is up against Jong-won.

813
00:30:53,718 --> 00:30:55,787
You can join as a team too. Yes?

814
00:30:55,854 --> 00:30:57,755
Nam-no's in.

815
00:30:57,822 --> 00:30:59,390
-But you know…
-Yes?

816
00:30:59,457 --> 00:31:00,959
…I want to be a team with someone.

817
00:31:01,025 --> 00:31:02,594
-As a team?
-A team round?

818
00:31:02,660 --> 00:31:04,629
You want to be a team with someone?

819
00:31:04,696 --> 00:31:06,464
-Who?
-With our grandpa.

820
00:31:06,764 --> 00:31:09,133
CHEF & MY FRIDGE'S GRANDPA

821
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:11,569
-I'm in dire need of winning a star.
-He needs stars.

822
00:31:11,636 --> 00:31:14,072
-Hence, Chef Hyun-suk.
-You should go with him to win.

823
00:31:14,138 --> 00:31:17,008
He's never lost a team round,
so I'd like to cook with him.

824
00:31:17,075 --> 00:31:19,811
-Nam-no wants you. Is that okay?
-Is that okay?

825
00:31:19,878 --> 00:31:21,813
I mean, I even did it with Sung-jun.

826
00:31:21,880 --> 00:31:24,148
-Right.
-Chef Nam-no?

827
00:31:24,215 --> 00:31:26,651
He'd be much easier to work with.

828
00:31:26,718 --> 00:31:28,453
He's like a hired star sniper.

829
00:31:28,519 --> 00:31:32,156
-He'd like to get on Hyun-suk's wagon.
-And win a star.

830
00:31:32,223 --> 00:31:35,760
So Nam-no and Hyun-suk are a team.

831
00:31:35,827 --> 00:31:38,029
People can join
as an individual or a team, right?

832
00:31:38,096 --> 00:31:41,599
-Of course. Two or even three.
-A team of three.

833
00:31:41,666 --> 00:31:42,867
-Three?
-Three chefs?

834
00:31:43,835 --> 00:31:45,003
How about four?

835
00:31:45,069 --> 00:31:47,272
-Let's go with four.
-Why not four?

836
00:31:47,605 --> 00:31:50,208
THE FIRST EVER 4:2 BATTLE?

837
00:31:50,842 --> 00:31:52,577
Then we'll recruit one more.

838
00:31:52,644 --> 00:31:54,545
YI MOON-JUNG RAISES HER HAND

839
00:31:54,612 --> 00:31:56,714
When it comes to eggplants,
it's Chinese food.

840
00:31:56,781 --> 00:31:58,449
That is true.

841
00:31:58,516 --> 00:32:01,052
-So I'd like to jump in with Eun-yeong.
-Chinese cuisine.

842
00:32:01,119 --> 00:32:03,221
The Chinese sisters will face them.

843
00:32:03,288 --> 00:32:07,025
-We didn't win in the last team round.
-You've done it.

844
00:32:07,091 --> 00:32:09,761
So I've always wanted
to cook with her again.

845
00:32:09,827 --> 00:32:10,929
-To restore your pride?
-Yes.

846
00:32:10,995 --> 00:32:12,096
Let's go, Eun-yeong.

847
00:32:12,363 --> 00:32:13,831
LET'S GO AND TAKE THEM DOWN!

848
00:32:13,898 --> 00:32:15,633
GETTING CHILLS

849
00:32:15,700 --> 00:32:18,536
Yi Moon-jung and Park Eun-yeong
joins the round.

850
00:32:18,603 --> 00:32:22,707
But first, let us begin the round
between Jong-won and Sung-jun!

851
00:32:25,677 --> 00:32:30,014
<i>This is the first cook-off</i>
<i>with ingredients from Ye-jin's fridge.</i>

852
00:32:30,081 --> 00:32:32,717
A chef who wishes
to make even guests laugh,

853
00:32:32,784 --> 00:32:34,052
Chef Son Jong-won,

854
00:32:34,118 --> 00:32:36,688
versus a chef
who wants clout even as a guest,

855
00:32:37,255 --> 00:32:40,291
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

856
00:32:40,358 --> 00:32:41,726
-"Clout"?
-Yes.

857
00:32:41,793 --> 00:32:44,429
As of now, Jong-won has won 12 times

858
00:32:44,495 --> 00:32:45,863
and Sung-jun has won eight times.

859
00:32:45,930 --> 00:32:46,998
-Yes.
-Whoa.

860
00:32:47,065 --> 00:32:48,099
More importantly,

861
00:32:48,166 --> 00:32:50,335
in their most recent battle,

862
00:32:50,401 --> 00:32:52,704
Sung-jun won against Jong-won.

863
00:32:52,770 --> 00:32:55,039
-He did?
-Yes.

864
00:32:55,106 --> 00:32:56,874
That was a big issue, right?

865
00:32:56,941 --> 00:32:59,777
I have to beat number one
to create a lot more buzz.

866
00:32:59,844 --> 00:33:00,678
I must win today.

867
00:33:00,745 --> 00:33:03,915
Even when you win, are you not happy
if you don't get enough attention?

868
00:33:03,982 --> 00:33:05,516
It's meaningless just to win.

869
00:33:05,883 --> 00:33:07,485
CLOUT IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN STARS

870
00:33:07,552 --> 00:33:09,654
You can always cook something buzzworthy.

871
00:33:09,721 --> 00:33:11,556
LIKE THESE

872
00:33:11,622 --> 00:33:13,891
Is there a reason
why you must get a star from Ye-jin?

873
00:33:13,958 --> 00:33:16,027
Actually, she has been

874
00:33:16,094 --> 00:33:20,398
my ideal type of a thousand years, so…

875
00:33:20,465 --> 00:33:22,000
-Since my childhood.
-Ideal type?

876
00:33:22,066 --> 00:33:24,502
An ideal type of a thousand years?
You aren't that old.

877
00:33:24,569 --> 00:33:26,104
You mean "a thousand tears"?

878
00:33:26,170 --> 00:33:30,074
She has been my ideal type
for 30 years, so I'm quite nervous.

879
00:33:30,141 --> 00:33:32,910
You two are going to make
a dish to entertain guests,

880
00:33:32,977 --> 00:33:36,180
and Chef Jong-won said
he felt quite confident.

881
00:33:36,247 --> 00:33:38,750
Even before I got into this line of work,

882
00:33:38,816 --> 00:33:41,219
I'd put together a menu and hand it out

883
00:33:41,285 --> 00:33:43,788
to my mother's guests when they visited.

884
00:33:43,855 --> 00:33:46,824
So I've been entertaining guests
for a very long time.

885
00:33:46,891 --> 00:33:49,127
I was in high school back then,

886
00:33:49,193 --> 00:33:51,462
which means Sung-jun
was probably in kindergarten.

887
00:33:52,597 --> 00:33:53,931
He must've been a little kid.

888
00:33:53,998 --> 00:33:56,134
What do you make
when your guests come over?

889
00:33:56,200 --> 00:34:00,171
I invite them to my restaurant instead.

890
00:34:00,238 --> 00:34:03,141
Sung-jun, do you not
have many guests at home?

891
00:34:03,207 --> 00:34:06,444
I'm not exactly a sociable person.

892
00:34:06,511 --> 00:34:07,745
You don't seem so.

893
00:34:07,812 --> 00:34:10,281
I've never been to anyone's place
or have them visit me.

894
00:34:10,348 --> 00:34:12,850
-So these occasions are a mystery…
-Right.

895
00:34:12,917 --> 00:34:14,852
…so I looked it up online.

896
00:34:15,620 --> 00:34:17,088
"What do I make for houseguests?"

897
00:34:17,155 --> 00:34:18,923
LOL

898
00:34:19,891 --> 00:34:22,193
Everything is from my imagination.

899
00:34:22,260 --> 00:34:24,929
He didn't join the round for the order.

900
00:34:24,996 --> 00:34:28,199
-He's here to leech off Jong-won.
-He can get attention

901
00:34:28,266 --> 00:34:29,934
if he's next to Jong-won.

902
00:34:30,001 --> 00:34:32,870
If your dish isn't good enough,
Ye-jin will get mad.

903
00:34:32,937 --> 00:34:35,239
He's not here for the star.

904
00:34:35,306 --> 00:34:36,374
You mentioned that

905
00:34:36,441 --> 00:34:41,145
you wanted some secrets
that can make you look smug, right?

906
00:34:41,212 --> 00:34:42,480
I'm all about the smugness.

907
00:34:42,547 --> 00:34:44,015
-That's true.
-Mr. Smug.

908
00:34:44,081 --> 00:34:46,317
So I'd like to share a few tips

909
00:34:46,384 --> 00:34:49,620
that'd make your guests go,
"She can do this?"

910
00:34:49,687 --> 00:34:53,224
It should be elegant enough for Ye-jin.
Can you do that?

911
00:34:53,291 --> 00:34:54,959
I can be smug elegantly.

912
00:34:56,060 --> 00:34:56,928
Please do that.

913
00:34:56,994 --> 00:34:59,297
Today's winner

914
00:34:59,363 --> 00:35:01,232
will get a star from Ye-jin

915
00:35:01,299 --> 00:35:04,869
and a cosmetics set
for your luxurious skincare experience.

916
00:35:05,103 --> 00:35:08,439
WOW

917
00:35:09,040 --> 00:35:11,109
You can use what Ye-jin uses at home.

918
00:35:11,175 --> 00:35:12,977
I need to do skincare at home.

919
00:35:13,044 --> 00:35:14,912
I want it too. I'll give it to my mom.

920
00:35:14,979 --> 00:35:18,449
You'll get that gift along with the star,
so please do your best.

921
00:35:19,283 --> 00:35:21,352
CHEF SUNG-JUN'S INGREDIENTS:
TOMATO MARINADE, BASIL PESTO

922
00:35:21,419 --> 00:35:23,454
HOMEMADE PATTIES, SIRLOIN,
BONELESS CHICKEN THIGHS, ABALONE, AND MORE

923
00:35:23,521 --> 00:35:26,124
Chef Sung-jun, what are you making?

924
00:35:26,190 --> 00:35:30,261
I've named my dish
after your most recent work I've enjoyed,

925
00:35:30,328 --> 00:35:31,629
<i>No Other Choice.</i>

926
00:35:31,696 --> 00:35:33,264
The name of my dish is

927
00:35:33,331 --> 00:35:34,799
No Losing Choice.

928
00:35:34,866 --> 00:35:36,634
-No Losing Choice?
-No Losing Choice.

929
00:35:36,701 --> 00:35:38,736
-He can't lose.
-No Losing Choice.

930
00:35:38,803 --> 00:35:40,438
-No Losing Choice.
-No Losing Choice.

931
00:35:40,505 --> 00:35:42,473
The peak of smugness.

932
00:35:42,540 --> 00:35:44,008
I'm a strategist.

933
00:35:44,075 --> 00:35:47,912
Hence, I thought about the kind of guests
she would have over the most often.

934
00:35:47,979 --> 00:35:50,448
-So I did some digging.
-"Digging"?

935
00:35:50,515 --> 00:35:52,984
I found that Hyun-bin's family

936
00:35:53,050 --> 00:35:55,653
is from South Jeolla-do,
around Gangjin, Damyang,

937
00:35:55,720 --> 00:35:58,923
-and Gwangju area.
-I see. Damyang.

938
00:35:58,990 --> 00:36:00,992
So if you made something from that area,

939
00:36:01,058 --> 00:36:03,461
your in-laws, Hyun-bin,

940
00:36:03,528 --> 00:36:05,663
or his friends would enjoy it even more.

941
00:36:05,963 --> 00:36:07,765
STRATEGIC

942
00:36:07,832 --> 00:36:09,400
There's a dish called <i>hoechun-tang.</i>

943
00:36:09,467 --> 00:36:11,669
-<i>Hoechun-tang?</i>
-It means "return to spring,"

944
00:36:11,736 --> 00:36:13,938
meaning that it'll make you younger.

945
00:36:14,005 --> 00:36:16,874
On the side, I'll make pot rice
because that shows care.

946
00:36:16,941 --> 00:36:20,211
But I'll add a little something
to help you be smug.

947
00:36:20,278 --> 00:36:21,212
Italian pot rice.

948
00:36:21,279 --> 00:36:23,181
-Italian pot rice?
-Italian pot rice?

949
00:36:23,247 --> 00:36:25,716
As far as I'm concerned,
I've never seen that in Italy.

950
00:36:25,783 --> 00:36:29,253
-What will you do?
-There's no pot rice in Italy.

951
00:36:29,320 --> 00:36:32,390
Your basil pesto
and tomato marinade were so delicious,

952
00:36:32,456 --> 00:36:34,725
-so I cannot <i>not</i> use them.
-Those?

953
00:36:34,792 --> 00:36:35,726
I just can't lose.

954
00:36:35,793 --> 00:36:38,496
-You're using those?
-For the pot rice?

955
00:36:38,563 --> 00:36:40,164
I'm going to do that.

956
00:36:40,998 --> 00:36:43,034
CHEF JONG-WON'S INGREDIENTS:
STRIP LOIN, ABALONE, OCTOPUS

957
00:36:43,100 --> 00:36:45,169
SHRIMP, CHINESE YAM, ZUCCHINI,
BURDOCK, HYUN-BIN'S HONEY, AND MORE

958
00:36:45,236 --> 00:36:48,472
Chef Jong-won, you're up against him.
What are you making?

959
00:36:48,539 --> 00:36:50,841
I'm going to make <i>ibaji </i>food.

960
00:36:50,908 --> 00:36:53,611
-What?
-<i>Ibaji</i> food?

961
00:36:53,678 --> 00:36:56,314
<i>These are the kinds of dishes</i>
<i>you make and serve to your neighbors</i>

962
00:36:56,380 --> 00:36:59,517
to do good for the people
when there's a happy occasion.

963
00:36:59,584 --> 00:37:01,118
I'm going to make some <i>jeon.</i>

964
00:37:01,185 --> 00:37:02,386
WOW♥

965
00:37:02,453 --> 00:37:04,255
Your son loves it too.

966
00:37:04,322 --> 00:37:06,057
When it comes to <i>jeon, </i>there is a master

967
00:37:06,123 --> 00:37:09,260
-named Ms. Kim Mae-sun.
-Ms. Kim Mae-sun.

968
00:37:09,327 --> 00:37:12,396
I'm making a <i>jeon</i> dish
which I learned from her.

969
00:37:12,463 --> 00:37:13,864
So my dish's called

970
00:37:13,931 --> 00:37:15,166
A <i>Buchimgae</i> to Remember.

971
00:37:15,233 --> 00:37:17,101
It's a perfect name.

972
00:37:17,168 --> 00:37:18,803
SOUNDS SO RIGHT

973
00:37:18,869 --> 00:37:21,105
-Man.
-Not a moment, but <i>buchimgae.</i>

974
00:37:21,172 --> 00:37:23,274
THAT WAS FUNNY

975
00:37:23,341 --> 00:37:24,742
I'll make <i>buchimgae,</i> a type of <i>jeon,</i>

976
00:37:24,809 --> 00:37:27,345
along with braised abalone
and octopus skewers.

977
00:37:27,411 --> 00:37:28,980
Octopus skewers?

978
00:37:29,046 --> 00:37:30,615
Can you do that in 15 minutes?

979
00:37:30,681 --> 00:37:33,184
I'll also give you a few tips
to let you cook elegantly

980
00:37:33,251 --> 00:37:35,853
-while I'm making the dishes.
-All right.

981
00:37:35,920 --> 00:37:39,924
Let us begin this round,
"Someone Who Cooks Well for Her Guests."

982
00:37:39,991 --> 00:37:41,792
A DISH TO ENTERTAIN GUESTS

983
00:37:41,859 --> 00:37:44,495
Chef Kwon Sung-jun is here,
determined to get a star

984
00:37:44,562 --> 00:37:47,765
from his ideal type of a thousand years.

985
00:37:47,832 --> 00:37:51,769
But Chef Son Jong-won is here
to erase Sung-jun

986
00:37:51,836 --> 00:37:54,338
-from all of Ye-jin's memories.
-I was never there.

987
00:37:54,405 --> 00:37:57,308
Which chef will do a better job
at entertaining guests?

988
00:37:57,375 --> 00:38:00,778
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

989
00:38:01,712 --> 00:38:02,580
EXCITED

990
00:38:03,281 --> 00:38:05,383
TIME TO START ENTERTAINING GUESTS

991
00:38:05,683 --> 00:38:06,851
WHAT'S THE MASTER'S SECRET?

992
00:38:06,917 --> 00:38:08,085
-It'll be a busy round.
-What's that? Flour?

993
00:38:08,152 --> 00:38:09,553
Let's take a look.

994
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:12,723
First, I'll toast the flour
to add a nutty flavor

995
00:38:12,790 --> 00:38:15,526
and to dry out any moisture
that might be inside.

996
00:38:15,593 --> 00:38:18,129
-Toast the flour.
-What are you doing with the pot?

997
00:38:18,195 --> 00:38:20,531
-I'm making rice.
-That's the pot.

998
00:38:20,598 --> 00:38:22,300
He's making the pot rice with that?

999
00:38:22,366 --> 00:38:24,201
With the tomato thing?

1000
00:38:24,268 --> 00:38:26,203
We've tried that side dish.

1001
00:38:27,605 --> 00:38:30,141
-Basil pesto too.
-Soy sauce.

1002
00:38:30,207 --> 00:38:32,043
-Is that soy sauce?
-Soy sauce.

1003
00:38:32,109 --> 00:38:34,745
He just put the pot rice on the heat.

1004
00:38:34,812 --> 00:38:36,614
-Will that turn out okay?
-Wait.

1005
00:38:36,681 --> 00:38:39,650
Is he using scissors
to be smug with elegance?

1006
00:38:40,284 --> 00:38:41,686
He's making seafood soup

1007
00:38:41,752 --> 00:38:43,754
-with very clear broth.
-Clear broth.

1008
00:38:43,821 --> 00:38:45,923
It's barely seasoned.

1009
00:38:45,990 --> 00:38:47,725
I see. Is that what you're doing?

1010
00:38:47,792 --> 00:38:48,893
That's right.

1011
00:38:48,959 --> 00:38:53,030
<i>Hoechun-tang</i> is usually made
with chicken and octopus.

1012
00:38:53,097 --> 00:38:56,634
But I'm using small octopuses
and dried pollack for the broth instead.

1013
00:38:56,701 --> 00:38:58,302
-It's a clear soup dish, then.
-Yes.

1014
00:38:58,369 --> 00:39:02,440
He's adding radish now
to make the soup refreshing.

1015
00:39:02,506 --> 00:39:04,508
-He added everything that's good.
-Wait.

1016
00:39:04,575 --> 00:39:08,012
The ingredients are all too big
for him to cook in 15 minutes.

1017
00:39:08,079 --> 00:39:09,613
Will those get cooked?

1018
00:39:09,680 --> 00:39:12,283
He should use
a pressure cooker or a steamer.

1019
00:39:12,350 --> 00:39:13,918
It's a clear soup, so it's fine.

1020
00:39:13,984 --> 00:39:15,553
-Is it okay?
-A clear soup.

1021
00:39:15,619 --> 00:39:17,188
We're now two minutes in.

1022
00:39:17,254 --> 00:39:19,290
Sung-jun takes
more attention at the beginning.

1023
00:39:19,724 --> 00:39:20,558
MINCED GINGER

1024
00:39:20,624 --> 00:39:24,128
He seems quite busy,
making several dishes.

1025
00:39:24,195 --> 00:39:26,230
2. MAKE BRAISING SAUCE WITH CONDIMENTS

1026
00:39:26,530 --> 00:39:27,398
AND HE'LL BRAISE…

1027
00:39:27,465 --> 00:39:28,299
Abalone.

1028
00:39:29,100 --> 00:39:30,935
-Briefly, over low heat.
-Briefly.

1029
00:39:31,001 --> 00:39:32,803
He blanched the abalone a little.

1030
00:39:32,870 --> 00:39:35,373
-I'm making braised abalone, <i>jeonbok-cho.</i>
-Braised abalone.

1031
00:39:35,439 --> 00:39:37,108
That's royal cuisine, right?

1032
00:39:37,174 --> 00:39:39,577
Yes, it's different from ordinary braises

1033
00:39:39,643 --> 00:39:41,979
because it's much thicker.

1034
00:39:42,046 --> 00:39:44,749
-I see.
-That's it. I've made the sauce already.

1035
00:39:44,815 --> 00:39:46,016
<i>Chopi</i>-infused <i>aekjeot.</i>

1036
00:39:46,083 --> 00:39:46,917
Take the bottle.

1037
00:39:46,984 --> 00:39:49,653
He's using
the prickly ash-infused fish sauce.

1038
00:39:50,421 --> 00:39:52,757
<i>Jeon</i> is all about pre-seasoning.

1039
00:39:52,823 --> 00:39:56,093
I've mixed <i>chopi</i>-infused <i>aekjeot</i>,
sesame oil, and ground pepper.

1040
00:39:56,160 --> 00:39:57,762
4. MIX CONDIMENTS WITH FISH SAUCE

1041
00:39:57,828 --> 00:39:59,930
He never started a conversation before,

1042
00:39:59,997 --> 00:40:01,799
but he's now asking us to talk to him.

1043
00:40:01,866 --> 00:40:03,734
Yes, we've only been talking to Sung-jun.

1044
00:40:03,801 --> 00:40:06,470
-He's looking for attention too.
-Yes.

1045
00:40:06,537 --> 00:40:07,571
Is he jealous?

1046
00:40:08,339 --> 00:40:10,508
This is why human greed is endless.

1047
00:40:10,808 --> 00:40:12,977
FIRST JEON, CHINESE YAM

1048
00:40:13,043 --> 00:40:15,346
-Chicken thigh meat, I think. Yes.
-Boneless?

1049
00:40:15,846 --> 00:40:16,781
It's chicken.

1050
00:40:18,015 --> 00:40:19,450
Is that for the soup?

1051
00:40:19,517 --> 00:40:22,987
It's originally made with a whole chicken,
but I can't be smug about that.

1052
00:40:23,053 --> 00:40:25,222
-Into skewers.
-Chicken skewers.

1053
00:40:25,289 --> 00:40:28,092
So I'll grill this first
and then present it nicely.

1054
00:40:28,159 --> 00:40:29,860
Then the adults would ask,

1055
00:40:29,927 --> 00:40:31,929
"Why did you do it like this, Ye-jin?"

1056
00:40:31,996 --> 00:40:34,064
It's an awesome tip.

1057
00:40:34,131 --> 00:40:36,400
You can tell them,
"They'll make you three years younger."

1058
00:40:36,467 --> 00:40:38,969
Yes, that they'd be younger
and would live longer.

1059
00:40:39,036 --> 00:40:41,639
-They'd live longer.
-Because it's chicken and green onion,

1060
00:40:41,705 --> 00:40:43,174
they'll flap like a healthy chicken.

1061
00:40:43,741 --> 00:40:45,109
IN DISBELIEF

1062
00:40:46,844 --> 00:40:48,446
3. GRILL CHICKEN AND GREEN ONION SKEWERS

1063
00:40:48,512 --> 00:40:50,014
It's been four minutes.

1064
00:40:50,080 --> 00:40:51,182
Wow.

1065
00:40:51,248 --> 00:40:52,349
Emergency.

1066
00:40:52,416 --> 00:40:53,484
It's an emergency.

1067
00:40:53,551 --> 00:40:54,685
I have so much to do.

1068
00:40:54,752 --> 00:40:58,889
Chef Sung-jun is using
the organic veggies she got as a present.

1069
00:40:58,956 --> 00:41:01,592
He's using the bell peppers
while Jong-won is using the zucchini.

1070
00:41:01,659 --> 00:41:03,494
He's making them look nice.

1071
00:41:03,561 --> 00:41:06,497
-He's making zucchini <i>jeon.</i>
-<i>Buchimgae.</i>

1072
00:41:06,564 --> 00:41:07,832
What did he put those into?

1073
00:41:07,898 --> 00:41:09,233
-The fish sauce.
-Fish sauce?

1074
00:41:09,300 --> 00:41:10,234
But why?

1075
00:41:10,301 --> 00:41:11,936
He's seasoning those.

1076
00:41:12,002 --> 00:41:15,539
You can season it evenly
when you dip it in liquid.

1077
00:41:15,606 --> 00:41:19,076
-Is that beef for the meat <i>jeon?</i>
-Yes.

1078
00:41:19,143 --> 00:41:22,179
Strip loin has lots of fat,
so I'm making the slices thin.

1079
00:41:22,246 --> 00:41:23,981
-I see.
-It's rather fatty.

1080
00:41:24,048 --> 00:41:26,383
It takes so much work to make <i>jeon.</i>

1081
00:41:26,450 --> 00:41:30,721
-He has to do so much.
-<i>Ibaji</i> food was mostly for weddings.

1082
00:41:30,788 --> 00:41:32,189
They all go into <i>aekjeot.</i>

1083
00:41:32,256 --> 00:41:33,457
5. DIP INGREDIENTS IN PRE-SEASONING

1084
00:41:33,524 --> 00:41:36,026
-They seem relaxed so far.
-He pulls them out right away.

1085
00:41:36,093 --> 00:41:37,328
ABALONE AND OCTOPUS, OUT

1086
00:41:37,828 --> 00:41:40,130
He's adding the grilled chicken
into the soup.

1087
00:41:40,197 --> 00:41:42,066
-After grilling them.
-Just like that?

1088
00:41:42,132 --> 00:41:45,870
It adds some more flavor
than adding it raw.

1089
00:41:45,936 --> 00:41:47,972
The soup, <i>hoechun-tang</i>, is boiling hard.

1090
00:41:48,038 --> 00:41:50,274
Huh? Are you getting just the soup?

1091
00:41:50,341 --> 00:41:52,209
-Just the foam.
-The foam.

1092
00:41:52,276 --> 00:41:54,578
But he's removing foam

1093
00:41:54,645 --> 00:41:57,681
only to pour it over the octopuses.

1094
00:41:57,748 --> 00:42:01,318
Maybe they're too tender,
so he wants them to taste dry.

1095
00:42:01,385 --> 00:42:03,020
He's adding the foam flavor.

1096
00:42:03,087 --> 00:42:04,822
Are you making them dry on purpose?

1097
00:42:04,889 --> 00:42:05,789
Correct.

1098
00:42:05,856 --> 00:42:07,491
-"Correct"?
-Hey.

1099
00:42:07,558 --> 00:42:09,527
-Did you even hear what we said?
-No.

1100
00:42:09,593 --> 00:42:10,928
Wait, what?

1101
00:42:10,995 --> 00:42:13,597
He's pushing bell pepper sticks
into the lotus root.

1102
00:42:13,664 --> 00:42:15,299
I'm making <i>jeon</i> as well, lotus root <i>jeon</i>.

1103
00:42:15,366 --> 00:42:16,734
and I wanted to add color.

1104
00:42:16,800 --> 00:42:19,737
-By sticking them in?
-And he'll chop those.

1105
00:42:19,803 --> 00:42:22,773
It does show lots of care.

1106
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:27,011
He could've coated the bell pepper pieces
with flour before sticking them in.

1107
00:42:27,077 --> 00:42:29,813
Yes, he only coated the lotus root.

1108
00:42:29,880 --> 00:42:32,116
-It's on the side.
-But nothing's inside.

1109
00:42:32,182 --> 00:42:34,585
-No one's on my side.
-We're unsure about that now.

1110
00:42:34,652 --> 00:42:36,820
But he's adding some fun points.

1111
00:42:36,887 --> 00:42:38,289
He's cutting it.

1112
00:42:38,355 --> 00:42:40,090
-It's falling out.
-The lotus root.

1113
00:42:40,157 --> 00:42:41,158
HOW WILL IT GO?

1114
00:42:41,225 --> 00:42:44,628
I hope the bell pepper pieces stay in.

1115
00:42:44,695 --> 00:42:47,164
Nice job.
He's finally coating it with flour.

1116
00:42:47,231 --> 00:42:49,199
-That's pretty.
-Those won't fall out now.

1117
00:42:49,266 --> 00:42:51,969
-The elders would love that.
-It might fall out while cooking.

1118
00:42:52,036 --> 00:42:54,405
You're making <i>jeon</i>
only with a coat of flour?

1119
00:42:54,471 --> 00:42:56,640
No, this is how it's done.

1120
00:42:56,707 --> 00:42:58,008
I doubt he's made <i>jeon</i> before.

1121
00:42:58,576 --> 00:42:59,743
That's true.

1122
00:42:59,810 --> 00:43:01,645
I prepped shrimp

1123
00:43:01,712 --> 00:43:04,048
while Sung-jun was chopping bell peppers,
and here's a tip.

1124
00:43:04,114 --> 00:43:07,117
Cut the front of the shrimp

1125
00:43:07,184 --> 00:43:09,954
<i>and turn it into a heart shape.</i>

1126
00:43:10,020 --> 00:43:11,455
It helps it cook evenly.

1127
00:43:11,522 --> 00:43:14,792
I've been working on the bell peppers
while Jong-won was prepping shrimp.

1128
00:43:14,858 --> 00:43:16,827
This will cook these evenly.

1129
00:43:16,894 --> 00:43:17,728
Bell peppers, I mean.

1130
00:43:17,795 --> 00:43:18,696
Is that so?

1131
00:43:19,563 --> 00:43:22,533
I think you can be
more smug with those shrimp.

1132
00:43:22,600 --> 00:43:24,301
He made hearts with shrimp.

1133
00:43:24,368 --> 00:43:25,569
Hearts?

1134
00:43:25,636 --> 00:43:27,538
People will be surprised to see those.

1135
00:43:27,605 --> 00:43:29,306
Unlike Chef Sung-jun,

1136
00:43:29,373 --> 00:43:32,910
Jong-won goes the traditional way
and uses flour and egg wash.

1137
00:43:32,977 --> 00:43:34,812
-That's the usual way.
-Yes, flour and egg wash.

1138
00:43:34,878 --> 00:43:36,413
Sung-jun did it differently.

1139
00:43:36,981 --> 00:43:37,848
This is how it's done.

1140
00:43:37,915 --> 00:43:39,483
He's using bell peppers for that too.

1141
00:43:39,550 --> 00:43:42,720
You said you liked a bit of texture.

1142
00:43:42,786 --> 00:43:43,954
So I'll add some bell peppers.

1143
00:43:44,688 --> 00:43:46,023
Are you using soy sauce?

1144
00:43:46,090 --> 00:43:48,425
I'll season it lightly with soy sauce
and then steam it.

1145
00:43:48,492 --> 00:43:50,361
-He'll steam it?
-Steam it?

1146
00:43:50,427 --> 00:43:52,663
-Instead of grilling?
-Steaming.

1147
00:43:52,730 --> 00:43:54,131
No starch or anything?

1148
00:43:54,198 --> 00:43:55,199
That looks good.

1149
00:43:55,399 --> 00:43:56,266
ZUCCHINI

1150
00:43:56,600 --> 00:43:57,835
REMOVING THE INSIDE

1151
00:43:57,901 --> 00:44:01,005
Sung-jun seems to be much faster.

1152
00:44:01,071 --> 00:44:03,407
And the pot rice is cooking right now.

1153
00:44:03,474 --> 00:44:05,309
-Something smells burnt.
-It's very…

1154
00:44:05,376 --> 00:44:06,644
Really?

1155
00:44:07,678 --> 00:44:08,512
I'll turn off the heat.

1156
00:44:09,313 --> 00:44:11,248
-Burdock?
-Burdock.

1157
00:44:11,315 --> 00:44:12,583
-Burdock.
-Burdock.

1158
00:44:12,650 --> 00:44:14,051
-Burdock.
-Burdock.

1159
00:44:14,118 --> 00:44:16,353
-Burdock.
-Burdock.

1160
00:44:16,420 --> 00:44:18,188
I'm making burdock tea.

1161
00:44:18,355 --> 00:44:20,090
WHOA

1162
00:44:20,157 --> 00:44:22,660
I'll make the tea
with soy sauce and honey.

1163
00:44:22,726 --> 00:44:26,230
That sounds so healthy.
That might make you even younger.

1164
00:44:26,296 --> 00:44:28,165
7. ROAST BURDOCK, ADD WATER,
THEN BRING TO A BOIL

1165
00:44:28,599 --> 00:44:30,234
Five minutes. Please go check, Sung-ju.

1166
00:44:30,300 --> 00:44:32,036
Let me report from the kitchen.

1167
00:44:32,102 --> 00:44:33,871
Jong-won versus Sung-jun.

1168
00:44:33,937 --> 00:44:35,239
Five minutes?

1169
00:44:35,839 --> 00:44:36,674
Emergency.

1170
00:44:36,740 --> 00:44:38,242
-Emergency.
-Emergency.

1171
00:44:38,308 --> 00:44:40,077
7. STUFF ZUCCHINI WITH SHRIMP AND EGG YOLK

1172
00:44:40,144 --> 00:44:41,712
He'll fill those with shrimp.

1173
00:44:43,847 --> 00:44:46,350
-Sung-jun's doing well today.
-It's boiling with dried pollack in it.

1174
00:44:46,417 --> 00:44:48,419
-Emergency. Whoa.
-Whoa.

1175
00:44:48,485 --> 00:44:51,955
-His pot rice is on the way.
-Shake it.

1176
00:44:52,022 --> 00:44:55,225
And this soup. <i>Hoechun-tang.</i>

1177
00:44:55,292 --> 00:44:56,860
With 4 minutes and 28 seconds left,

1178
00:44:56,927 --> 00:44:58,896
let me try the soup.

1179
00:44:58,962 --> 00:45:01,231
It looks too hot. Now, <i>hoechun-tang.</i>

1180
00:45:01,565 --> 00:45:02,700
SLURP

1181
00:45:02,766 --> 00:45:04,201
Does it taste like he won't lose?

1182
00:45:04,768 --> 00:45:06,837
I think he will.

1183
00:45:07,404 --> 00:45:08,572
What's it like?

1184
00:45:08,639 --> 00:45:11,241
-I haven't seasoned it yet.
-I only taste pollack.

1185
00:45:11,308 --> 00:45:13,343
I haven't seasoned it yet.

1186
00:45:13,410 --> 00:45:16,013
Jong-won is making <i>buchimgae.</i>

1187
00:45:16,080 --> 00:45:18,048
It takes so much work to make <i>jeon.</i>

1188
00:45:18,115 --> 00:45:20,150
He must have a lot to do

1189
00:45:20,217 --> 00:45:21,785
to serve many different dishes.

1190
00:45:22,086 --> 00:45:23,554
SUNG-JUN BEGINS TO GRIDDLE JEON TOO

1191
00:45:23,620 --> 00:45:27,024
-He filled the zucchini with something.
-Shrimp. Zucchini and shrimp <i>jeon.</i>

1192
00:45:27,091 --> 00:45:29,326
-Zucchini and shrimp?
-Zucchini and shrimp <i>jeon.</i>

1193
00:45:29,393 --> 00:45:30,928
They're both making <i>jeon.</i>

1194
00:45:30,994 --> 00:45:33,964
The difference in <i>jeon</i>
may become the decisive point.

1195
00:45:34,031 --> 00:45:35,766
What's this?

1196
00:45:35,833 --> 00:45:38,702
-Braised abalone, <i>jeonbok-cho.</i>
<i>-Jeonbok-cho.</i>

1197
00:45:38,769 --> 00:45:41,238
-He's adding button mushrooms.
-Those are probably

1198
00:45:41,805 --> 00:45:43,373
the topping.

1199
00:45:43,440 --> 00:45:46,810
Jong-won's busy using three stoves.

1200
00:45:46,877 --> 00:45:49,713
Let me try the braising sauce
for the abalone.

1201
00:45:50,280 --> 00:45:51,648
What's this like?

1202
00:45:51,715 --> 00:45:52,583
Braised abalone.

1203
00:45:53,417 --> 00:45:56,053
Will she give him the star
after eating that?

1204
00:45:56,887 --> 00:45:58,922
-No?
-It's not too deep yet.

1205
00:45:58,989 --> 00:46:00,224
-Gosh.
-No.

1206
00:46:00,290 --> 00:46:03,393
-It needs deeper flavors.
-Really?

1207
00:46:03,460 --> 00:46:05,062
You have two and a half minutes.

1208
00:46:05,129 --> 00:46:07,097
Two minutes and thirty seconds.

1209
00:46:07,164 --> 00:46:08,732
He grabs a small octopus.

1210
00:46:08,799 --> 00:46:10,000
WRAPPING IT AROUND A WOODEN CHOPSTICK

1211
00:46:10,234 --> 00:46:11,769
OCTOPUS SKEWER

1212
00:46:11,835 --> 00:46:13,604
He's making the skewer now.

1213
00:46:13,670 --> 00:46:14,671
That looks so good.

1214
00:46:14,738 --> 00:46:17,241
That looks good. Gosh.

1215
00:46:17,307 --> 00:46:19,009
What was her name? Kim Mae…

1216
